{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check && git status --short --untracked-files=all\ntail -1 \"$TMPDIR/bench-formal.drXNM35h/formal-result.log\"\nsed -n '\"'1,120p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\nFormal: 53 passed, 0 failed\n# Shared EX address addition and divider staging\n\n- `alu.sv` exports the raw modulo-2^32 sum. The ALU ADD result and the EX/MEM effective-address register both use that sum. The existing EX/MEM stall holds the effective address and store payload together.\n- EX adds the low two bits of forwarded `rs1` and the immediate for JALR. Bit 1 controls the alignment trap, link eligibility, and indirect redirect. Fetch uses the registered full sum for the accepted, word-aligned target, and RVFI records the resolved successor.\n- The native divider updates its magnitude, sign, normalization, product, and correction registers every edge. The synchronous seed ROM is read every edge. The controller holds the launch operands through completion and qualifies the result after preparation and seven updates. The existing reset, consume, and ALTOPS timing still apply.\n- The ALU unit test also checks the raw sum for each vector, including vectors that select another ALU operation.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Strict Verilator lint and all seven cocotb suites passed. Divider vectors exercise held completion, reset cancellation, divide-by-zero, and signed overflow. Pipeline tests check the ten-cycle divide retirement bound.\n- Single-channel RVFI cosimulation passed for selftest and all three random programs in ready and stalled modes. Stalled CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs and completed 10 iterations in 3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles.\n- Gowin placements 0, 1, and 2 measured 86.477, 93.810, and 93.810 MHz, with 4,152 logic cells and 1,465 registers. The median yields 237.13 CoreMark iterations/s, a 5.16% gain over 225.49 iterations/s. The worst setup paths in placements 1 and 2 run from MEM/WB forwarding to the registered indirect recovery select.\n- The full single-channel ALTOPS gate passed all 53 checks on the first run, including liveness and depth-20 register consistency. Formal and cosimulation used unmodified verification sources staged under the task scratch directory.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
