{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat formal/wrapper_si.sv\ncat formal/checks_si.cfg\ncat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "// riscv-formal wrapper for single-issue (`nret 1`) cores. Same shape and\n// memory model as wrapper.sv, but instantiates `core` with only the\n// channel-0 RVFI port set — no `_1` ports. The single-issue path produces\n// only ch0 checks; checks_si.cfg sets `nret 1` so genchecks does not\n// emit any `_ch1` tasks (no PREUNSAT-as-pass tally needed). Selected by\n// the orchestrator when cores/<target>/core.yaml declares nret: 1.\n`include \"rvfi_macros.vh\"\n\nmodule rvfi_wrapper (\n    input clock,\n    input reset,\n    `RVFI_OUTPUTS\n);\n    // Solver picks any 32-bit instruction each cycle.\n    (* keep *) `rvformal_rand_reg [31:0] imem_data;\n\n    // 8 KiB dmem (2048 words).\n    reg [31:0] dmem [0:2047];\n\n    wire [31:0] imem_addr;\n    wire [31:0] dmem_addr;\n    wire [31:0] dmem_wdata;\n    wire [3:0]  dmem_wen;\n    wire        dmem_ren;\n\n    always @(posedge clock) begin\n        if (dmem_wen[0]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][7:0]   <= dmem_wdata[7:0];\n        if (dmem_wen[1]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][15:8]  <= dmem_wdata[15:8];\n        if (dmem_wen[2]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][23:16] <= dmem_wdata[23:16];\n        if (dmem_wen[3]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][31:24] <= dmem_wdata[31:24];\n    end\n\n    core uut (\n        .clock              (clock),\n        .reset              (reset),\n        .io_imemAddr        (imem_addr),\n        .io_imemData        (imem_data),\n        .io_imemReady       (1'b1),\n        .io_dmemAddr        (dmem_addr),\n        .io_dmemWData       (dmem_wdata),\n        .io_dmemRData       (dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]]),\n        .io_dmemWEn         (dmem_wen),\n        .io_dmemREn         (dmem_ren),\n        .io_dmemReady       (1'b1),\n        // Channel 0 — the only retirement channel. With nret=1 in\n        // checks_si.cfg, every rvfi_* signal here is sized for a single\n        // channel, so the core's `_0` ports connect directly without\n        // slicing. No `_1` ports exist on the core's IO under nret=1.\n        .io_rvfi_valid_0    (rvfi_valid),\n        .io_rvfi_order_0    (rvfi_order),\n        .io_rvfi_insn_0     (rvfi_insn),\n\n…[10534 of 13606 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e5374b5dda887550]…\n parallel PC+4 and B/J replay RAM reads after the read edge left the registered direct-recovery select path into the predicted fetch PC limiting median Gowin Fmax to 75.288 MHz, below the 80.90 MHz required to clear the acceptance margin with 7.78% fewer stalled CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r9s0 (improvement, +5.16%): Holding divider launch operands through completion and qualifying results with done let the divider drop its stage enables to clear the seed-ROM-to-product timing limit, while sharing the pre-mux ALU ADD sum reduced forwarding fanout and a separate two-bit sum kept JALR recovery selection independent of the full address sum, raising median Gowin Fmax from 89.207 to 93.810 MHz.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r9s1 (regression, +3.28%): Banking EX/MEM results preserved next-cycle bypass and CoreMark’s 3,956,104 timed cycles, and the exposed JALR recovery path from `effective_addr` to `predicted_pc_q` capped median Gowin Fmax at 92.135 MHz, below the 93.31 MHz acceptance threshold.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
