{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_38", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; r=json.loads(open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\").readlines()[26]); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"", "aggregated_output": "{'motivation': 'The champion scores 225.49 CoreMark iterations per second at 89.207 MHz\\nand 3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles. The placement-option-0 report\\nshows a 17.099 ns path from MEM/WB write_value through the operand mux\\nand integer ALU into EX/MEM alu_result. That destination also sits\\nbehind selection for link and RV32M results. Earlier MEM-stage RV32M\\nlanes incurred a dependent issue bubble. Capturing each ready result\\nbefore the shared selection can shorten EX and keep next-cycle bypass.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Replace the unified EX/MEM write-result register with three parallel\\nregistered lanes for ADD/ADDI/SUB/AUIPC, other base integer writers,\\nand JAL/JALR/RV32M. Decode the one-hot result-lane tag in ID, carry it\\nwith the producer, and capture the lane results at the existing EX/MEM\\nedge. For the add/subtract lane, use an ID-registered subtract bit to\\ncomplement operand B and seed the carry for subtraction. Form carry-in\\nzero and carry-in one candidates locally in each 8-bit block. Compute\\ngroup carries in parallel from block propagate and generate signals,\\nthen select each byte. Use the existing ALU operand choices for PC,\\nimmediate and register inputs. This gives the sum a short path from\\noperand forwarding to its own register, with modulo-2^32 wraparound.\\n\\nOn ID acceptance, resolve producer age first and register a one-hot\\nmask selecting the future EX/MEM lane, the future MEM/WB write value,\\nor the captured register-file value for each raw source. Form the masked\\nEX/MEM lane terms in parallel with the MEM/WB and register-file terms\\nand OR them in the EX operand mux. This keeps any ready EX result\\navailable to the very next instruction. Keep the PC and immediate\\noverrides in the registered ALU operand choices. In MEM, combine the\\nmasked lanes and the formatted load as parallel sources of the existing\\nMEM/WB write-value register. Loads still first forward from MEM/WB.\\nA completed divide still commits from EX/MEM and supplies the same lane\\nvalue for commit and forwarding.\\n\\nP\n…[1289 of 4361 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c09fbf9a8b7a2a2e]…\n carry-select blocks for add/subtract. Preserve the existing standalone ALU op, a, b and out interface and operation semantics.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Capture independent ALU and link/RV32M results and the matching lane tag in EX/MEM. Apply the registered lane masks directly to the operand candidates while preserving EX/MEM hold, RV32M launch and consume, and forwarding eligibility.'}, {'file': 'rtl/forward_unit.sv', 'description': \"Encode the winning EX writer's lane in each raw-source mask after resolving producer priority. Retain MEM/WB and register-file fallback and exclude ineligible and x0 writes.\"}, {'file': 'rtl/mem_stage.sv', 'description': 'Select the EX/MEM lane or load value for the registered MEM/WB bypass, and take early divide writeback from the link/RV32M lane.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Wire the result lanes and producer tag into execute, forwarding and memory while retaining the current memory handshake and single-channel RVFI commit arbitration.'}]}\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
