{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_168", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && git status --short && sed -n '1,38p' implementation_notes.md\"", "aggregated_output": " M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\n# Pipeline branch resolution past issue\n\nOrdinary conditional branches compare forwarded operands in EX. EX/MEM holds the comparison and one-hot recovery enables alongside the sequential and direct candidates. Each candidate carries its fetch-window, last-word, and direction-index metadata. IF selects the registered correction while the core discards speculative ID/EX work and admits the corrected response. JAL, JALR, and non-branch boundary recovery still trigger from EX.\n\nA legal BEQ or BNE can issue immediately behind a matching load with a nonzero destination. The branch saves its ordinary forwarded operands and both source-match bits. In MEM it compares against the formatted load value in MEM/WB if the load wrote its destination. A trapped load leaves the saved operands in effect. Both matching sources compare equal. The MEM result controls the branch's next PC, trap, and RVFI operands.\n\nA registered late correction suppresses both younger slots, including their memory requests, backpressure, bypasses, arithmetic launch, and branch training. MEM preserves the retiring branch for one cycle to use the freed WB slot. It also retains the load bypass value across a surviving branch so a deferred divide can still use its original source selection.\n\nOrdinary predictor updates can reach the pending counter at the EX edge when no older update is waiting. This preserves predictor visibility for a corrected fetch that aliases the branch index. Otherwise the update follows the branch into EX/MEM. IF prioritizes older updates and queues the younger update when two surviving branches resolve together.\n\nThe timing iterations moved ordinary recovery qualification onto the EX/MEM edge and selected the direction index in par\n…[1354 of 4426 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7bee02380c0c6197]…\nelow use required clock period in nanoseconds from Gowin's setup report. No path launched by the late-correction register appeared in the 25 reported paths for any selected placement. The table gives the period of the last reported path for reference. Before the late target mux moved into MEM, placement 0 reported a 12.182 ns path from the late correction register to the IF queue occupancy.\n\n| Place option | Fmax MHz | Ordinary correction to fetch PC | Forwarded base ALU | Forwarded low multiply | Last reported path |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | 87.643 | 11.342 | 11.407 | 11.410 | 11.211 |\n| 1 | 90.538 | 11.030 | 11.044 | 11.045 | 10.885 |\n| 2 | 90.538 | 11.030 | 11.044 | 11.045 | 10.885 |\n\nThe median is 90.538 MHz and the resulting fitness is 235.35 iterations/s, 0.75% below the champion. The measured cycles require 95.418 MHz for the 4.6% acceptance margin. The forwarded low multiply is the longest reported path in all three placements, with base ALU and ordinary recovery close behind.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
