{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_51", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -m 3 'hyp-20261121-003-r5s2|formal_seconds|formal_elapsed' cores/bench/experiments; sed -n '1,140p' tools/eval/formal.py\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261121-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Decouple predicted fetch with a five-entry queue and registered recovery\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The 154.30 iter/sec champion needs 4,190,307 timed CoreMark cycles at\\n64.657 MHz. The previous runahead predictor used otherwise idle fetch\\ncycles to save 5.57% of the timed cycles, but its median clock of\\n59.469 MHz erased the advantage against the current champion. Isolate\\npredictor runahead from both late EX branch recovery and the decode\\nhandshake so fetch can cover stalls while preserving EX timing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The 154.30 iter/sec champion needs 4,190,307 timed CoreMark cycles at\\n64.657 MHz. The previous runahead predictor used otherwise idle fetch\\ncycles to save 5.57% of the timed cycles, but its median clock of\\n59.469 MHz erased the advantage against the current champion. Isolate\\npredictor runahead from both late EX branch recovery and the decode\\nhandshake so fetch can cover stalls while preserving EX timing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Feed a five-entry circular fall-through queue from the champion's\\n64-entry folded-PC direction predictor. Each slot is a 95-bit\\nregister holding the 30-bit word PC, instruction, predicted-taken\\nbit, and full 32-bit B/J target, including the halfword bit used\\nfor traps. Retain the split PC-relative target sum in IF and carry\\nthe selected prediction and target through the existing ID/EX\\npayload. Give aligned in-window direct jumps their target on the\\nfirst visit. Predict JALR sequentially.\\n\\nRotate one-hot read and write pointers independently and track\\noccupancy with a five-bit unary count. Use a masked OR of the\\nselected physical slot and bypass the array when empty. Pop only\\nwhen decode admits the head. Fetch whenever imem is ready and\\nthere is already a free slot, even if decode is held. A full queue\\nfrees a slot on a pop and resumes fetching on the following cycle.\\nThe fifth slot limits the cost of this policy while removing the\\ncombinational \n…[12653 of 15725 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0ba3988a009374ed]…\ni.cfg\n                so the single-issue formal flow runs instead of the\n                default dual-channel one.\n\n    Returns:\n      {'passed': True, 'checks_passed': N}\n      {'passed': False, 'failed_check': name, 'detail': str}\n    \"\"\"\n    worktree_path = Path(worktree).resolve()\n    run_script = worktree_path / \"formal\" / \"run_all.sh\"\n    if not run_script.exists():\n        return {'passed': False, 'failed_check': 'setup',\n                'detail': f'formal/run_all.sh missing in {worktree}'}\n\n    env = _build_formal_env(worktree_path, target)\n    # Pin the work dir name so exactly this run's dir is removed in\n    # `finally`, once the tally (or failing-check log tail) is captured.\n    work_id = \"h\" + uuid.uuid4().hex[:15]\n    env = {**env, \"FORMAL_WORK_ID\": work_id}\n    try:\n        try:\n            from tools.eval._slots import eval_jobs, eval_slot\n            if eval_jobs():\n                env = {**env, \"JOBS\": eval_jobs()}\n            # The ceiling starts once a host eval slot is held (_slots.py).\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
