{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check; sed -n '1,100p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Bench branch target buffer\n\n## Implementation\n\n- IF uses a 64-entry direct-mapped BTB indexed by PC[7:2] with a full PC[31:8] tag, a word-aligned target, and a two-bit saturating counter. Reset clears entry validity. Tag, target, and counter arrays use the same fetch index and infer RAM in Gowin.\n- EX trains advancing legal control transfers. A taken branch allocates a weakly taken counter on a miss, strengthens it on a hit, and records the resolved target. A matching not-taken branch weakens the counter while preserving the last taken target. JAL and JALR install strongly taken entries. A same-index write forwards the tag match and new counter to the registered training metadata. The current prediction comes from the pre-edge lookup.\n- Hazard logic uses a single fetch-accept signal for the IF PC update, predictor metadata capture, and ID/EX admission. An older dmem stall holds all three and defers training and recovery. EX compares its full resolved next PC with the current fetch address for every advancing valid instruction. A mismatch bubbles ID/EX and redirects even with imem unready. RVFI and recovery use the same EX PC resolution.\n- The IF PC register stores the upper 30 bits and supplies the two aligned low bits at the interface. ID/EX establishes validity on admission directly. The EX/MEM bundle carries the resolved PC used for retirement.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all five cocotb runners passed. The directed pipeline tests cover bubble-free taken prediction, loop-exit and changing-JALR recovery across both bus stalls, wrong-path side effects, and the same-index training bypass followed by a stale non-control prediction.\n- Local single-issue formal passed all 53 checks on the first attempt.\n- Cosim matched selftest and all three random programs with and without both bus stalls. CoreMark passed the canonical CRC checks with both stalls enabled.\n\n## Timing and fitness\n\n- CoreMark measured 4,186,330 marker-bracketed cycles for 10 iterations, a 6.79% reduction from 4,491,485.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 5.240, 5.533, and 5.533 MHz. Option 0 used 12,183 logic cells. The median yields 13.22 iterations/second, about 9.0% above 12.12.\n- The worst setup path crosses operand forwarding and the ALU before reaching dmem. Placement option 0 measured below 5.31 MHz, so the placement median matters for fitness.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
