26-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r2s0 (improvement, +13.61%): Registered high-product correction, a parallelized four-bit divider seed, and wrong-path squashes via ID/EX validity shortened EX arithmetic and redirect-to-load-use feedback paths, raising median Gowin Fmax from 48.196 to 54.759 MHz while keeping CoreMark’s hot MUL single-cycle and preserving its \n…[2754 of 5826 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f90aa31554e880ec]…\n the round winner.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r5s0 (regression, +8.83%): Moving bypass comparisons into ID and registering the selected MEM/WB write value raised median Gowin Fmax from 64.657 to 70.367 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles, with same-cycle forwarding into the divider launch seed limiting all three placements.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r5s1 (regression, +8.85%): Registering final one-hot bypass selects in ID moved the median Gowin timing limit from divider launch to divider recurrence for just a 0.010 MHz gain over registering matches alone, so with unchanged CoreMark cycles it lost to the five-entry fetch queue that cut timed cycles by 5.59%.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r5s2 (improvement, +9.48%): Squashing ID/EX immediately, selecting the corrected fetch address from registered recovery targets, and refilling only when a slot was already free broke the combinational EX-to-IF redirect and decode-to-refill paths while the five-entry fall-through queue cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 5.59%.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
