{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -B -c 'import json; es=[json.loads(x) for x in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\")]; print(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\".join(f\\\"{e[chr(105)+chr(100)]}\\\\n{e.get(chr(105)+chr(109)+chr(112)+chr(108)+chr(101)+chr(109)+chr(101)+chr(110)+chr(116)+chr(97)+chr(116)+chr(105)+chr(111)+chr(110)+chr(95)+chr(110)+chr(111)+chr(116)+chr(101)+chr(115))}\\\" for e in es if e.get(\\\"round_id\\\")==5 and e.get(\\\"slot\\\") in (0,1)))'\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20261121-001-r5s0\n# Register bypass matches before execute\n\n## Implementation\n\n- ID/EX captures a nonzero EX and MEM destination match for each raw source field alongside the register-file operands. The tags share the ID/EX hold and validity rules. The load-use interlock still checks both raw fields, including immediate bits that alias a register.\n- EX qualifies each match with the corresponding staged, post-trap write control. Three mutually exclusive masks select EX/MEM, MEM/WB, or the captured register-file value. A trapped younger writer leaves an eligible older writer available to either operand.\n- MEM registers the selected architectural write value after load sign or zero extension. WB uses that value for both forwarding and the register-file port. A data wait holds ID/EX and EX/MEM and retains the MEM/WB write value and producer metadata. Clearing MEM/WB retirement valid prevents another retirement or register-file write.\n- The forwarding selector is instantiated inside EX so it consumes the tags directly from the ID/EX bundle. High multiply and divide reuse EX's existing launch-time operand registers for RVFI, and the M units sample the same resolved operands on launch. The existing native and ALTOPS arithmetic schedules apply.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Strict Verilator lint passed in native and ALTOPS modes.\n- `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the first run, including register consistency, liveness, and unique retirement.\n- All seven cocotb runners passed, covering 86 cases. The five added pipeline cases check dual-writer priority and x0, fallback after a misaligned JALR, a load-use bubble on a raw rs2 alias, a retained load value across three stalled store cycles, and launch-time forwarding into DIV and MULH. They check exact RVFI operand values, contiguous retirement order, and accepted store requests where applicable.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1` matched the ISS for selftest and three random programs, with ready buses and with both stall generators enabled. Stalled CoreMark completed in 4,190,307 marker-bracketed cycles, passed all five canonical CRCs, and reported `Correct operation validated.`\n\n## Gowin timing\n\n| Placement option | Fmax | Worst setup arrival |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | 69.952 MHz | 19.590 ns |\n| 1 | 70.367 MHz | 19.505 ns |\n| 2 | 70.367 MHz | 19.505 ns |\n| Median | **70.367 MHz** | |\n\nPlacement 0 reported 3,378 logic cells and 766 registers. The measured median and 10 CoreMark iterations yield **167.93 iterations/s**, up **8.83%** from 154.30 iterations/s. The median clears the 67.631 MHz acceptance threshold and falls below the scratch prototype's 72.846 MHz. The longest setup path in each placement ends at the divider launch seed. It starts at the MEM/WB write-value register in placement 0 and at the ID/EX rs1 match in placements 1 and 2.\n\n\nhyp-20261121-002-r5s1\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
