{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_105", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; from pathlib import Path; rows=[json.loads(l) for l in Path(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\").read_text().splitlines()]; [print(r[\\\"id\\\"],r.get(\\\"formal_passed\\\"),r.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\")) for r in rows if r[\\\"id\\\"] in (\\\"hyp-20261121-003-r6s2\\\",\\\"hyp-20261121-003-r7s2\\\",\\\"hyp-20261121-003-r8s2\\\")]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r6s2 True # Resolved-backedge loop replay\n\nIF tracks one inclusive loop of up to 128 words. EX signals an advancing, aligned, taken conditional backedge and IF captures its PC and target independently. A contained backedge keeps the current descriptor. Ready bus words stage for refill into a synchronous block RAM containing the instruction, page bit, and 18-bit prediction target with its eligibility bit. Replay checks the full page address, the indexed word's page bit and validity, and any read/write collision.\n\nThe three-entry distributed RAM queue carries the word PC, instruction, and taken prediction. Bus and replay share the folded direction table and supply separate target candidates. EX computes the full architectural direct target and supplies resolved RVFI next PCs. A replay that advances while imem is unready holds the outstanding request address through its ready response. IF discards that response and resumes requests at the producer PC. Registered EX recovery overrides the held address, squashes younger work, and disables replay in the corrected cycle.\n\nAn accepted store overlapping the descriptor clears word validity. FENCE.I clears the descriptor and any pending refill, then restarts fetch at its sequential successor. The directed tests cover replay across two 512-byte pages, draining and overriding held requests, ordered retirement on loop exit, and a store patching a warmed loop while a younger FENCE.I waits behind the store.\n\n## Timing changes\n\nThe first placements exposed long paths through the replay collision check and EX forwarding. The final RTL records the RAM write index and compares it with the registered producer index in the following cycle. It also computes last-word parking eligibility without waiting on the bus target sum. Decode registers the raw source-to-destination matches and EX qualifies them with the post-trap write enables. MEM registers the selected architectural write value before forwarding. These timing changes preserve the instruction and arithmetic issu\n…[7252 of 10324 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ab7af47710756ffd]…\ns, and the illegal-instruction check. `reg_ch0` remained in progress at handoff with no counterexample reported.\n\nThe formal and co-simulation commands use scratch copies of the existing harnesses and benchmark inputs. The formal wrapper ties both readiness inputs high. The directed tests and stalled co-simulation exercise replay with changing readiness.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\n| Placement | Fmax (MHz) | Worst setup path |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| 0 | 79.285 | Direct replay block RAM to the ID/EX operand through the register file |\n| 1 | 75.288 | Registered direct recovery select to predicted fetch PC |\n| 2 | 75.288 | Registered direct recovery select to predicted fetch PC |\n\nThe median was 75.288 MHz. At 3,648,511 bracketed cycles, that yields 206.35 iterations/sec, 2.65% below the champion. The 4.6% acceptance margin requires 80.90 MHz at this cycle count. The selected implementation had the highest median of the locally measured variants. It used 5,170 logic cells, 2,006 registers, nine block RAMs and nine DSPs.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
