anges [{'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': 'Export the raw 32-bit sum and use the same sum in the existing ALU_ADD case. Preserve the other ALU operations.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Capture the raw ALU sum in the existing effective address register. Replace the separate full-width address adder with a two-bit JALR alignment sum and preserve stalls, traps, and recovery.'}, {'file': 'rtl/div_unit.sv', 'description': 'Update the native feed-forward pipeline registers on every edge and keep the synchronous seed ROM read enabled. Keep the launch operands held and retain the completion, cancel and consume schedule.'}]\noutcome\n…[24107 of 27179 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9b8cd9923e94e48c]…\nx supported by Yosys.\n- Ready and stalled RVFI cosim passed for selftest and all three random programs. The local Make invocation used `NRET=1` from `core.yaml`. Stalled CoreMark passed the canonical CRC checks and completed 10 iterations in 3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles without out-of-bounds accesses.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 90.031, 95.220, and 95.220 MHz. The 95.220 MHz median yields 240.69 CoreMark iterations/s, about 1.50% above the champion. The median is below the 98.125 MHz acceptance threshold. Forwarded ALU result selection and fetch-state feedback remain the limiting paths.\n\ntimestamp 2026-11-21T20:47:51.598892Z\nslot 1\nround_id 10\nlesson - 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r10s1 (regression, +1.50%): Preparing page-split PC+4 and B/J targets in ID and registering one-hot recovery requests preserved CoreMark's 3,956,104 timed cycles, leaving forwarded ALU result selection and fetch-state feedback to cap median Gowin Fmax at 95.220 MHz against the 98.125 MHz acceptance threshold.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
