{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git status --short && rg -n --hidden -g '\"'!.git'\"' -g '\"'!.codex-home/**'\"' -g '\"'!formal/riscv-formal/**'\"' 'hyp-20261121-003-r2s2|116\\\\.68|4\\\\.6% acceptance|4,186,330' cores/bench\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261121-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict hot control transfers with a single-read tagged target buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The bench core fetches fall-through until EX resolves a control transfer. Each\\naligned taken branch or jump normally discards one fetch opportunity because\\nIF feeds ID/EX without a separate IF/ID register. CoreMark repeatedly visits\\nloop branches, calls, and returns. The baseline's 5.442 MHz median Fmax is\\nlimited by the forwarded ALU path, so the predictor must earn its gain in\\nmarker-bracketed cycles while keeping the fetch feedback path short.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench core fetches fall-through until EX resolves a control transfer. Each\\naligned taken branch or jump normally discards one fetch opportunity because\\nIF feeds ID/EX without a separate IF/ID register. CoreMark repeatedly visits\\nloop branches, calls, and returns. The baseline's 5.442 MHz median Fmax is\\nlimited by the forwarded ALU path, so the predictor must earn its gain in\\nmarker-bracketed cycles while keeping the fetch feedback path short.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 64-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer indexed by fetch PC[7:2],\\nwith a full PC[31:8] tag, a word-aligned target, and a two-bit saturating\\ndirection counter. A valid tag hit with the counter's high bit set selects\\nthe stored target in parallel with the existing PC+4 calculation. Learn\\ntaken conditional branches and JAL/JALR from EX, using the most recent\\nresolved target. Initialize a newly allocated conditional entry to weakly\\ntaken and strengthen or weaken matching entries on outcomes. Install legal\\naligned jumps as strongly taken. Train once per advancing valid EX control\\ninstruction and suppress training for traps. Reset the valid bits and leave\\nthe payload arrays unreset to allow distributed RAM inference.\\n\\nUse only the fetch-indexed table read. On an accepted fetch, register that\\ninstruction's lookup hit and counter alongside the IF PC update for use by\n…[7475 of 10547 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d6c4d1c234258505]…\nh bus stalls. CoreMark passed the canonical CRC checks with both stalls enabled.\\n\\n## Timing and fitness\\n\\n- CoreMark measured 4,186,330 marker-bracketed cycles for 10 iterations, a 6.79% reduction from 4,491,485.\\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 5.240, 5.533, and 5.533 MHz. Option 0 used 12,183 logic cells. The median yields 13.22 iterations/second, about 9.0% above 12.12.\\n- The worst setup path crosses operand forwarding and the ALU before reaching dmem. Placement option 0 measured below 5.31 MHz, so the placement median matters for fitness.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-21T13:38:45.936015Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r1s2 (regression, +9.08%): Benchmark fetch predictors on the current champion RTL because the single-read tagged BTB saved 6.79% of CoreMark timed cycles by avoiding taken-transfer bubbles, a gain outweighed by the older combinational DIV/REM ALU limiting median Fmax to 5.533 MHz against the iterative-divider champion\\u2019s 48.196 MHz.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
