{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-00[12]-r(9|10)s[01]' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; fields=(\\\"id\\\",\\\"category\\\",\\\"title\\\",\\\"critical_path\\\",\\\"seeds\\\",\\\"perturbations\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\"); [print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(f\\\"{k}: {d.get(k)}\\\" for k in fields), \\\"\\\\n\\\") for l in sys.stdin for d in [json.loads(l)]]'\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-001-r9s0\ncategory: micro_opt\ntitle: Share the EX address adder after simplifying divider staging\ncritical_path: {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_sel_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/select_indirect_q_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.564, 'arrival_ns': 16.858, 'logic_cells': 37, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if']}\nseeds: [86.477, 93.81, 93.81]\nperturbations: [[0, 86.477, 13], [1, 93.81, 13], [2, 93.81, 13]]\nimplementation_notes: # Shared EX address addition and divider staging\n\n- `alu.sv` exports the raw modulo-2^32 sum. The ALU ADD result and the EX/MEM effective-address register both use that sum. The existing EX/MEM stall holds the effective address and store payload together.\n- EX adds the low two bits of forwarded `rs1` and the immediate for JALR. Bit 1 controls the alignment trap, link eligibility, and indirect redirect. Fetch uses the registered full sum for the accepted, word-aligned target, and RVFI records the resolved successor.\n- The native divider updates its magnitude, sign, normalization, product, and correction registers every edge. The synchronous seed ROM is read every edge. The controller holds the launch operands through completion and qualifies the result after preparation and seven updates. The existing reset, consume, and ALTOPS timing still apply.\n- The ALU unit test also checks the raw sum for each vector, including vectors that select another ALU operation.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Strict Verilator lint and all seven cocotb suites passed. Divider vectors exercise held completion, reset cancellation, divide-by-zero, and signed overflow. Pipeline tests check the ten-cycle divide retirement bound.\n- Single-channel RVFI cosimulation passed for selftest and all three random programs in ready and stalled modes. Stalled CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs and completed 10 iterations in 3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles.\n- Gowin placements 0, 1, and 2 measured 86.477, 93.810, and 93.810 MHz, with 4,152 logic cells and 1,465 registers. The median yields 23\n…[6758 of 9830 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9e97679a946c8679]…\ns. All seven cocotb runners passed, including all 35 pipeline cases.\n- The unmodified single-channel formal suite passed 52 of 53 checks on this revision, including liveness and both PC checks. `reg_ch0` was still solving at the local time limit, with no counterexample reported. An earlier recovery implementation passed all 53 checks after changing the JALR helper to use the function assignment syntax supported by Yosys.\n- Ready and stalled RVFI cosim passed for selftest and all three random programs. The local Make invocation used `NRET=1` from `core.yaml`. Stalled CoreMark passed the canonical CRC checks and completed 10 iterations in 3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles without out-of-bounds accesses.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 90.031, 95.220, and 95.220 MHz. The 95.220 MHz median yields 240.69 CoreMark iterations/s, about 1.50% above the champion. The median is below the 98.125 MHz acceptance threshold. Forwarded ALU result selection and fetch-state feedback remain the limiting paths.\n \n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
