{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-00[12]-r(7|8|9|10)s[01]' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; fields=(\\\"id\\\",\\\"hypothesis\\\"); [print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(f\\\"{k}:\\\\n{d.get(k)}\\\" for k in fields), \\\"\\\\n\\\") for l in sys.stdin for d in [json.loads(l)]]'\"", "aggregated_output": "id:\nhyp-20261121-001-r7s0\nhypothesis:\n{'motivation': 'The bench champion scores 192.82 CoreMark iter/s at 76.281 MHz median\\nand 3,956,104 timed cycles. In Gowin placement 0 the setup path from\\nthe EX/MEM JALR address into the next predicted PC arrives at 18.558 ns,\\nwithin 6 ps of the reported divider path. Fetch currently selects the\\nrequest PC, hashes it for the prediction index, and qualifies predicted\\ntargets by reducing the upper bits of the computed target. Computing\\nthose two side signals in parallel cuts logic off the fetch address\\nrecurrence while preserving the prediction and recovery schedule.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Split PC-relative target arithmetic at bit 20, the boundary of the\\n1 MiB instruction window. Add the low 18 word-address bits to the low\\n18 bits of the signed B or J word offset. The carry minus the offset\\nsign adjusts PC[31:20] by +1, 0 or -1. Form the full target from the\\nthree page candidates and the low sum. In parallel, compare the\\noriginal page with 0xfff, 0 or 1 and combine those comparisons with\\nthe same adjustment to tell whether the target page is zero, including\\n32-bit wraparound. Keep the target halfword alignment check. Use the\\nsame page split for PC+4 and reuse the incremented page candidate.\\n\\nIn parallel with the request address mux, compute the existing\\nsix-bit XOR hash for the predicted PC and each registered recovery\\nPC. Select the hash with the same mutually exclusive masks as the\\nrequest address and use it for the existing direction lookup and\\npending-update check. Keep full 32-bit target arithmetic, predictor\\npolicy and updates, fetch queue admission, last-word parking and EX\\nrecovery priority. Preserve the current bus and retirement schedule.\\n\\nThe scratch build measured 80.204, 83.632 and 83.632 MHz across Gowin\\nplacement options 0, 1 and 2 with 4,089 logic cells in option 0.\\nLow-word multiply became the longest reported path. Stalled CoreMark\\npassed the canonical CRC checks in 3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles.\\nThe measured median and cycle cou\n…[31899 of 34971 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8038b4f2072a230d]…\n and capture them with the same ID/EX hold and validity rules.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Resolve the prepared cases into separate one-hot requests. Compute JALR alignment from the low bits of each forwarding candidate and retain the full effective address, branch training, link and RVFI successors.'}, {'file': 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'description': 'Capture one-hot correction requests directly and use them to select the registered targets and their window flags. Preserve queue reset, address holding and parking semantics.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Wire the request enables separately into IF and combine them for the immediate younger-ID squash. Preserve the current memory and RVFI interfaces.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Exercise recovery for both branch outcomes, indirect targets from all bypass sources, blocked corrected requests, and a misaligned boundary JALR followed by a divide. Check exact retirement order and absence of wrong-path effects.'}]} \n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
