{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-00[123]-r(4s2|5s2|10s1|11s2)\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json; [(print(d[\\\"id\\\"]),print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"])) for d in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r4s2\nRun the champion's 64-entry direction predictor at the producer of a\nfour-entry fall-through queue. Advance the fetch PC along the predicted\npath when a ready instruction is accepted directly by decode or enqueued.\nStore each instruction, its full PC, its six-bit lookup index and the bit\ndescribing the successor IF selected. Use a shift queue\nwith a fixed head and one occupancy bit per slot. Bypass it when empty so\na ready fetch still reaches ID/EX on the same edge. Pop only on actual\nID/EX admission. Retain the head on a load-use bubble or EX hold and allow\na simultaneous pop and refill at any occupancy, including full.\n\nUse separate asynchronous direction-table reads for fetch prediction and\nthe issuing instruction's training snapshot. Keep the issuing index mux\nindependent of the predicted-direction mux. Read the queue head into a\nscalar before accessing its fields for Yosys compatibility. Use the existing\nfolded PC index and forward a concurrent same-index update into the\nsnapshot while keeping the fetch prediction based on the pre-edge table.\nTrain only on a valid advancing legal conditional branch. Keep the\nprediction bit paired with the enqueued instruction. Reconstruct the full\ndirect target in EX from the registered PC and decoded immediate, in\nparallel with operand forwarding and the branch comparison. Use the\nimmediate's bit 1 to check direct-transfer alignment since the PC is word\naligned. Keep the one-bit mismatch check and derive the authoritative RVFI\nnext PC from the architectural resolution.\n\nIF only needs to form an aligned in-window target. For a source PC in\nthe 1 MiB window, add its zero-extended 18-bit word index to the signed\n19-bit B/J word offset in 19 bits. A clear result bit 18 certifies the\ntarget is in range. Use the offset's halfword bit to qualify alignment.\nOutside the window or when the candidate is invalid, predict fall-through\nand let EX resolve the full architectural successor.\n\nAn advancing EX correction clears queue occupancy and squashes decode even\nif \n…[10641 of 13713 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ec4bc70b2acc1e38]…\nght, and code replacement followed by\nFENCE.I with a warm replay tag. Check retirement PCs, order and\nwrong-path stores under randomized bus readiness.\n\nThe best scratch prototype passed canonical CoreMark validation in\n3,558,019 timed cycles, a 10.06% reduction. Gowin placements 0, 1 and\n2 measured 84.780, 91.566 and 91.566 MHz. The median gives 257.35\niter/sec, 8.53% above the champion. Its critical path moved to MEM/WB\nforwarding through the EX ALU. It also passed stalled RVFI cosim and\ndirected replay and FENCE.I tests. Its full formal run exceeded the\nscratch time limit. A Boolean rewrite of replay admission passed\nliveness at the same cycle count and reached 84.061 MHz, limited by\nindirect recovery into the fetch last-word flag. At the measured\ncycles the implementation needs a median above 88.25 MHz. Keep the\nbus, replay and recovery address and boundary-flag candidates in\nparallel through their final selects. Reproduce all three Gowin\nplacements and run the unmodified formal and stalled RVFI checks.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
