th. Stalled CoreMark\npassed the canonical CRC checks in 3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles.\nThe measured median and cycle count imply 211.40 iter/s, 9.64 percent\nabove the champion. The acceptance floor at this cycle count is\nabout 79.79 MHz. The scratch RTL passed the existing cocotb suites,\nthe directed window-edge test and selftest plus three random ISS\ntraces with ready and stalled buses. All 53 single-retire ALTOPS\nformal checks passed. A combinational SAT check proved the target,\nrange, increment and hash equations \n…[11607 of 14679 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5d6f28a2e54a79c4]…\n during recovery.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Strict Verilator lint and all seven cocotb runners passed. The pipeline runner covered 41 scenarios, including the ten-cycle divide retirement test.\n- Selftest and all three random programs passed RVFI cosimulation with and without stalls. Stalled CoreMark completed 10 iterations in 3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles with the canonical CRCs.\n- Gowin placements 0, 1, and 2 measured 72.883, 89.456, and 89.456 MHz. The 89.456 MHz median yields 226.12 CoreMark iterations/s and falls below the 98.125 MHz acceptance threshold. All three placements report the worst path from the ID/EX PC through the PC+4 logic to the IF sequential recovery window flag. The fetch feedback and ALU result paths are nearly tied in placements 1 and 2.\n- The unmodified single-channel formal gate passed 52 of 53 checks, including liveness and both PC checks. The depth-20 register consistency check (`reg_ch0`) was still solving when local verification ended. The solver reported no counterexample.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
