tening divider feedback raises the clock limit.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give the EX/MEM register a dedicated effective-address field fed by\n  forwarded rs1 plus the decoded immediate. Drive the LSU address,\n  alignment checks, lane masks and load extraction from that field. This\n  removes the ALU and multiplier result mux from the memory address path\n  while keeping MUL single-cycle. Hold the address and store payload together\n  under data bus backpressure.\n\n  Shorten the remaining divider feedback while preserving its eight wait\n  cycles. Keep the four-bit launch seed. On the first iteration, \n…[1840 of 4912 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:00ca1a23ce2afc33]…\n   description: |\n      Keep the four-bit issue seed and seven iteration edges. On the first\n      iteration use the raw partial dividend and divisor magnitude to finish\n      the next four quotient bits at eight-bit width, while capturing a\n      34-bit 3D. Use two parallel-trial radix-4 steps per subsequent edge,\n      widening each subtract to retain the borrow. Preserve all signedness,\n      zero-divisor and overflow results and the ALTOPS handshake schedule.\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise the narrow-to-wide iteration boundary, including divisor\n      magnitudes 255 and 256, zero and large unsigned divisors, and compare\n      all four DIV and REM results with the existing bit-exact reference.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Use a forwarded base and a signed offset on byte and halfword accesses.\n      Check the bus address and store payload across dmem stalls, the accepted\n      byte mask and load data, and exactly one RVFI retirement per access.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
