ns, 156-cell path through\\noperand forwarding and the EX ALU. The signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder operators are the likely source of that depth. Their outputs feed\\nthe same registered result used for forwarding and data addresses, so their\\ntiming limits the clock for every instruction. A baseline retirement trace\\nwith both bus stalls enabled reproduced the 4,491,485 timed cycles \n…[71582 of 74654 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7931bdf8a7526234]…\nn took 4,527,760 bracketed cycles, up 0.81% from the champion. The measured fitness was **138.83 iterations/s**, up 13.87%. Every placement exceeded the stated 57.74 MHz bar. Placement 0 used 2,961 logic cells, 667 registers, and two DSPs. The worst setup paths crossed the integer ALU into the data-address path.\\n\\nAn immediate dependency on a low multiply adds one issue bubble, including a dependency through a raw RVFI source field. High multiply adds one MEM wait cycle. Divide and remainder retain eight extra MEM cycles and hold younger issue during that time.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-21T14:32:40.560155Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r3s1 (regression, +13.87%): Registering forwarded RV32M operands in EX/MEM kept independent MUL throughput at one per cycle and shifted the critical path to integer address generation (54.759 to 62.861 MHz median Gowin Fmax), with a one-cycle issue interlock on either raw RVFI source alias and a 0.81% increase in timed CoreMark cycles.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
