{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_92", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfor f in [66.584,62.673,62.562,60.976]:\n s=f*1e7/4190307\n print(f, s, (s/138.94-1)*100)\nPY\nsed -n '1,130p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r4s0.yaml; git status --short\"", "aggregated_output": "66.584 158.90005195323397 14.365950736457433\n62.673 149.56660693357313 7.648342402168651\n62.562 149.301709874718 7.457686681098319\n60.976 145.51678433107645 4.733542774633981\nid: hyp-20261121-001-r4s0\ntitle: \"Shorten the EX address and divider carry paths\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current bench champion runs 10 CoreMark iterations in 4,190,307 timed\n  cycles at 58.220 MHz median. Its reported worst setup path reaches data\n  memory through the forwarded EX ALU and shared result selection. A scratch\n  split-address prototype moved the limit to divider launch and feedback and\n  reached 58.821 MHz. Shortening the divider carry path as well is needed to\n  turn the address timing improvement into a higher core clock.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a dedicated full-width effective-address register to EX/MEM. For\n  loads and stores, add forwarded rs1[11:0] and the low 12 bits of the\n  already decoded I- or S-type immediate, producing the low sum and carry.\n  Compute the unchanged, incremented, and decremented rs1[31:12] in\n  parallel. Select the increment when carry is set and the offset is\n  positive or zero, the decrement when carry is clear and the offset is\n  negative, and the unchanged value otherwise. This implements the exact\n  32-bit modulo sum for both offset signs. Feed the address register\n  directly from that sum, bypassing the execution result mux. Use its\n  address consistently for the bus, alignment, lane masks, load extraction\n  and RVFI. Hold it with the request and store payload through dmem stalls.\n\n  Keep the divider's four-bit launch seed and seven iteration edges. On\n  the first iteration, consume four more bits with eight-bit restoring\n  steps and register the 34-bit value 3D in parallel. A divisor of 256\n  or more cannot take any of the four quotient bits on that edge. On\n  each of the remaining six edges, use two radix-4 steps. In each step,\n  subtract D, 2D, and 3D from the shifted remainder in parallel using\n  35-bit differences. Select the difference from the largest multiple\n  that fits by masking the candidate remainders with mutually exclusive\n  predicates. Preserve divide-by-zero and signed-overflow results, signed\n  remainder, and the ALTOPS schedule and held-completion protocol.\n\n  The scratch implementation measured 57.130, 60.976, and 60.976 MHz in\n  the three Gowin placements. At 4,190,307 timed cycles, the median yields\n  145.52 CoreMark iter/sec, 4.73 percent above the champion. The margin\n  above the 60.90 MHz acceptance threshold is small, so retain the\n  split carry and one-hot remainder selection in the implementation.\n  Preserve the champion's forwarding schedule, branch prediction, load-use\n  interlock, and single-cycle low MUL.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a full-width effective-address field to the EX/MEM payload.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Form the signed-immediate address with a 12-bit low add and parallel upper candidates. Capture it on the existing EX/MEM edge with the store payload, using the same reset and hold behavior.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use the dedicated effective address for the data bus, alignment traps, lane selection and aligned RVFI reporting. Keep the architectural result path for forwarding and register writes.\"\n  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace the four wide restoring subtracts with two radix-4 steps per edge. Use an eight-bit first iteration and register 3D then. Keep the existing launch seed, signed result correction and eight-cycle wait in hardware and ALTOPS.\"\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: \"Compare all four operations with exact reference results at divisor magnitudes 255 and 256, with large unsigned divisors, and while completion is held.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Exercise forwarded bases with I- and S-type offsets crossing a 4 KiB boundary in both directions, including byte lanes, alignment traps and a store held by dmem backpressure.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
