{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_104", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r8s0.yaml && sed -n '490,575p' tools/agents/hypothesis.py\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-001-r8s0\ntitle: \"Shorten the fetch address recurrence with parallel carry logic\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The bench champion scores 211.98 CoreMark iterations per second at\n  83.861 MHz median Gowin Fmax and 3,956,104 timed cycles. Its longest\n  reported setup path starts at the registered sequential recovery select\n  and ends at the fetch parking register. The current last-word decision\n  waits for the general B/J target adder and prediction qualification even\n  though the source address is fixed for that decision. The same adder\n  also feeds the next request PC on taken predictions.\nhypothesis: |\n  Shorten the fetch address recurrence by separating the in-window\n  prediction address from the full architectural target. Detect the last mapped\n  word separately on the predicted PC and on each registered recovery\n  candidate, then combine the matches with the same one-hot selects used\n  for the request. At the last mapped word, a legal aligned B/J target is\n  in the window exactly when its offset is zero or negative, excluding\n  JAL's most-negative offset, which wraps to 0xfffffffc. Derive this\n  predicate from the immediate bits in parallel with direction lookup and\n  feed it directly into the existing boundary admission and parking logic.\n\n  Split the 18-bit in-window word sum into two nine-bit groups. Compute\n  both local sums, the upper group's carry generate and propagate, and\n  the lower carry in parallel. Use the lower carry to finish the upper\n  sum. Compute page-zero, page-one, and page-all-ones matches on each PC\n  candidate before address selection, and then use the final carry and\n  offset sign to qualify the prediction. Only a word-aligned page-zero\n  target can be predicted, so drive its high page and alignment bits\n  from constants on the predicted-PC input. Keep the full 32-bit wrapped\n  target on the queue and EX recovery path, including misaligned targets.\n\n  Preserve the request, enqueue, squash, and predictor training schedule\n  cycle for cycle, including last-word ad\n…[4501 of 7573 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e2ab4370c03f8363]…\nged_since({}, dirty_state(ws),\n                                  lambda p: p.startswith(f\"cores/{target}/experiments/hypotheses/\"))\n        if leftovers:\n            print(f\"  [agent] {hyp_id}: discarding workspace edits {leftovers[:6]}\", flush=True)\n        for f in ws_hyp_dir.glob(f\"{hyp_id}*.y*ml\"):\n            shutil.copy2(f, hyp_dir / f.name)\n    finally:\n        destroy_worktree(ws_id, target)\n\n    breaches = _git_offlimits_changes(allow_re, before)\n    if breaches:\n        # Hard-revert anything the agent touched outside its allow list\n        # (tools.sandbox.revert_paths: tracked -> HEAD, new -> deleted,\n        # pre-existing dirt left alone).\n        #\n        # NEVER revert the log.jsonl path — it's the orchestrator's own\n        # journal. If an agent's tool somehow leaves a touch-mark on it\n        # (file mtime change, no-content-diff write), the breach check\n        # picks it up and `git checkout HEAD -- log.jsonl` would throw\n        # away in-flight append_log writes, leaving the saved log\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_103","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'def test_|def run|build_dir|RTL_DIR' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py cores/bench/test/conftest.py && sed -n '360,420p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\"","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py:910:def test_pipeline_runner():\n    rets, _ = await _run(dut, program, fetch_requests=fetches)\n    assert fetches[:3] == [(0, True), (8, True), (12, True)]\n    pcs = [r[\"pc\"] for r in rets]\n    assert pcs == [0, 8, 12], f\"unexpected retirement PC sequence: {pcs}\"\n    [rj] = _by_insn(rets, JAL(1, 8))\n    assert rj[\"rd\"] == 1\n    assert rj[\"rd_wdata\"] == rj[\"pc\"] + 4\n    [r3] = _by_insn(rets, ADDI(3, 0, 42))\n    assert r3[\"rd_wdata\"] == 42\n    assert rj[\"pc_next\"] == 8 and r3[\"cycle\"] == rj[\"cycle\"] + 1\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def predicted_loop_exit_waits_for_older_store(dut):\n    loop_branch = BNE(1, 0, -20)\n    program = [\n        ADDI(1, 0, 2),          # 0\n        JAL(0, 24),             # 4: enter loop at its branch\n        ADDI(1, 1, -1),         # 8: predicted loop target\n        BEQ(1, 0, 12),          # 12: issue the store just before loop exit\n        JAL(0, 12),             # 16: back to the branch without the store\n        SW(1, 0, 0x48),         # 20: skipped\n        SW(1, 0, 0x44),         # 24: older store stalls with the branch in EX\n        loop_branch,            # 28\n        ADDI(2, 1, 42),         # 32: loop exit\n        EBREAK(),\n    ]\n    fetches, stores = [], []\n    rets, dmem = await _run(dut, program, fetch_requests=fetches, store_stalls=3,\n                           store_requests=stores,\n                           unready_fetches={(28, 4): program[5], (32, 1): program[5]})\n\n    assert [r[\"pc\"] for r in rets] == [0, 4, 28, 8, 12, 16, 28, 8, 12, 24, 28, 32, 36]\n    assert [r[\"order\"] for r in rets] == list(range(len(rets)))\n    assert [r[\"pc_next\"] for r in _by_insn(rets, loop_branch)] == [8, 8, 32]\n    assert rets[-2][\"rd_wdata\"] == 42\n    assert stores == [(0x44, 15, 0, False)] * 3 + [(0x44, 15, 0, True)]\n    assert dmem == {0x44: 0}\n\n    branch_fetches = [i for i, request in enumerate(fetches) if request == (28, True)]\n    assert len(branch_fetches) == 3\n    assert [fetches[i + 1] for i in branch_fetches[:2]] == [(8, True)] * 2\n    exit_fetch = branch_fetches[-1]\n    assert fetches[exit_fetch + 1:exit_fetch + 8] == [\n        (8, True), (12, True), (16, True), (28, False), (32, False), (32, True), (36, True),\n    ]\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def aliased_branches_bypass_training_snapshot(dut):\n    # The XOR index is 32 at 0x80, 0x1ffc, and 0x2000. The adjacent\n    # branches at 0x1ffc and 0x2000 can train/read on the same edge.\n    program = [NOP()] * (0x2028 // 4)\n    for pc, insn in {\n        0x0000: JAL(0, 0x80),\n        0x0080: BEQ(0, 0, 8),             # cold forward: 01 -> 10\n        0x0084: SW(0, 0, 0x40),          # squashed by the cold branch\n        0x0088: JAL(0, 0x1FFC - 0x88),\n        0x1FFC: BNE(0, 0, 2),             # untaken, unaligned target: 10 -> 01\n        0x2000: BEQ(1, 0, 0x20),          # taken once: bypassed 01 -> 10\n        0x2004: ADDI(2, 2, 1),\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
