{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-00[12]-r(2s0|4s0|5s0|6s0|7s0|7s1|8s0|8s1)\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; es=list(map(json.loads,sys.stdin)); note=lambda e:(e.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\") or \\\"\\\") if e[\\\"id\\\"].endswith((\\\"r8s0\\\",\\\"r8s1\\\")) else (e.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\") or \\\"\\\")[:1000]; [print(e[\\\"id\\\"], e[\\\"hypothesis\\\"], {k:e[k] for k in (\\\"fitness\\\",\\\"fmax_mhz\\\",\\\"cycles\\\",\\\"critical_path\\\",\\\"seeds\\\",\\\"perturbations\\\")}, note(e), sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\") for e in es]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r2s0\n{'motivation': 'The current bench champion scores 107.31 at a median 48.196 MHz and\\n4,491,485 timed CoreMark cycles. In placement option 0 the worst path\\ncrosses the signed high-product DSP and arrives at 26.329 ns. The divider\\nfeedback path is nearly tied at 26.309 ns. A stalled CoreMark retirement\\ntrace counted 93,960 MUL instructions between the markers and zero high\\nmultiplies, divisions, or remainders. A scratch prototype that shortened\\nboth arithmetic paths exposed a third limit. Redirect drove the fetch\\ninstruction mux, which fed back through load-use detection into the PC\\nand ID/EX controls. Median Fmax stopped at 49.128 MHz.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Shorten the long paths through EX while preserving the cycle behavior\\nof ordinary instructions. Give RV32M arithmetic registered intermediates.\\nFeed the forwarded register operands directly into one unsigned 32-by-32\\nproduct. Its low word feeds the existing single-cycle MUL result path.\\nFor MULH, MULHSU, and MULHU, capture the unsigned high word at launch\\nalong with the correction sum. The correction is rs2 when rs1 is signed\\nand negative, plus rs1 when rs2 is signed and negative, modulo 2^32.\\nRegister the high-word subtraction result on the following cycle. Seed\\nthe divider remainder and quotient on launch by processing the four most\\nsignificant dividend magnitude bits in a narrow datapath. For a negative\\ndividend, form the magnitude prefix by complementing the high nibble and\\nadding one if the low 28 bits are zero. Determine whether the divisor\\nmagnitude fits in four bits from the raw operands. A negative divisor is\\nsmall when its upper 28 bits are all ones and its low nibble is nonzero.\\nFor a divisor magnitude of at least 16, use the dividend prefix as the\\nseed remainder and zero as the seed quotient. Then run seven groups of\\nfour restoring steps on the remaining bits at the current divide latency.\\nUse the existing EX wait and completion policy for the high multiplies\\nand divides, including held results and retaine\n…[45938 of 49010 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6ccecdf4793eb98f]…\nnd the ten-cycle JALR-to-divide retirement gap.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator strict lint passed. All seven cocotb runners passed, with 94 cases including 31 pipeline cases.\n- Ready and stalled single-channel RVFI co-simulation passed. Stalled CoreMark validated all expected CRCs and completed 10 iterations in 3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles.\n- The single-channel ALTOPS formal gate passed all 53 checks, including liveness and register consistency. No formal fixes were needed.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 84.712, 89.207, and 89.207 MHz. The median is 1.487 MHz above the 87.72 MHz acceptance floor. This yields 225.49 CoreMark iterations/s, 6.37% above the supplied champion score.\n- The worst path in placement 0 crosses the forwarded integer ALU. The worst paths in placements 1 and 2 run from the divider seed ROM to the reciprocal product.\n\nFormal and co-simulation used scratch copies of the original verification files. The local co-simulation build used `NRET=1` to match `core.yaml`.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
