{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-002-r(7s1|8s1)\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; [print(e[\\\"id\\\"],e[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\") for e in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'; rg -n 'div_seed_rom|divide|latency' cores/bench/test/test_div_unit.py\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-002-r7s1\n# Goldschmidt divider\n\nThe bench divider captures forwarded operands at launch, captures magnitudes and signs at preparation, then completes on update seven. Its datapath uses a synchronous 1,024-entry reciprocal ROM, two fixed-point refinements, and a residual comparison that chooses between the provisional quotient and its successor. The remainder candidates apply the dividend sign by ordering the subtraction operands. Divide by zero returns an all-ones quotient or the original dividend. Signed overflow preserves the low 32-bit quotient.\n\nThe denominator deficit takes bits `[34:10]` of the negated product. This is equivalent to complementing the upper slice and adding one when the lower ten bits are zero. The second numerator refinement stores only bits `[34:3]` because denormalization always discards at least three bits. Bounding the seed error at the lower endpoint of each ROM interval and accounting for the Q34 truncations bounds the real quotient underestimate below 0.884 for every nonzero divisor. The exact comparison therefore needs at most one increment.\n\nThe first placements exposed timing paths in fetch and low MUL. Fetch now tests the high address candidates for zero in parallel with the target calculation. EX selects low MUL at the final result mux and retains its one-cycle EX schedule. The divider handshake retains completion hold and reset cancellation, the ALTOPS schedule, forwarded RVFI operands, and early single-channel retirement.\n\n## Gowin and CoreMark\n\n| Placement option | Fmax |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 79.652 MHz |\n| 1 | 83.861 MHz |\n| 2 | 83.861 MHz |\n| Median | **83.861 MHz** |\n\nStalled CoreMark completed 10 iterations in **3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles** with all five canonical CRCs and `Correct operation validated.` The median yields **211.98 iterations/s**, up **9.94%** from 192.82 and 4.070 MHz above the required median. Placement 0 used 4,317 logic cells, 1,625 registers, 40 LUT RAMs, 5 block RAMs, and 9 DSPs. Its longest reported setup path runs from fetch reco\n…[3586 of 6658 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9dcc7539abdf91dd]…\n divider seed ROM to the reciprocal product.\n\nFormal and co-simulation used scratch copies of the original verification files. The local co-simulation build used `NRET=1` to match `core.yaml`.\n\n1:\"\"\"RV32M arithmetic, latency and handshake tests for the clocked divider.\"\"\"\n79:    dividend, divisor = a & MASK32, b & MASK32\n81:        dividend = dividend - (1 << 32) if dividend >= 1 << 31 else dividend\n84:        quotient = abs(dividend) // abs(divisor)\n85:        if (dividend < 0) != (divisor < 0):\n87:        remainder = dividend - quotient * divisor\n89:        quotient, remainder = MASK32, dividend\n94:async def back_to_back_divides(dut):\n149:            for dividend in (multiple - 1, multiple, multiple + 1):\n150:                if dividend <= MASK32:\n152:                        await _check(dut, op, dividend, divisor,\n153:                                     _reference(op, dividend, divisor))\n194:async def reset_cancels_divide(dut):\n207:               sources=[\"core_pkg.sv\", \"div_seed_rom.sv\", \"div_unit.sv\"],\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
