{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_78", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r10s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-002-r10s1\ntitle: Prepare branch recovery context at decode\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The bench champion scores 237.13 CoreMark iterations/sec at 93.810 MHz\n  median Gowin Fmax and 3,956,104 timed cycles. In both median placements,\n  the worst setup path crosses the forwarded branch comparison, the\n  shared prediction mismatch, and the indirect request selector. The\n  round-nine result banks exposed JALR target feedback as another limit.\n  A separate recovery path must shorten both the execute decision and\n  the handoff to fetch while retaining the current redirect latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Prepare a recovery context in ID/EX beside the operand masks. From\n  validated decode, the prediction bit, displacement alignment, and the\n  last-mapped-word flag, register whether a taken branch needs the direct\n  request, a fall-through branch needs the sequential request, an aligned\n  JAL needs the direct request, or a boundary instruction needs forced\n  sequential recovery. Carry full-width PC+4 and PC-relative target\n  candidates from ID, with the displacement sign and halfword bit kept\n  for training and traps. These candidates are available at the start of\n  EX for link generation, architectural successors, and IF's recovery\n  address and window registers.\n\n  Resolve each request independently in EX. A conditional branch uses\n  its forwarded comparison to qualify only its two prepared outcomes.\n  JALR has its own request enable because IF predicts indirect jumps\n  sequentially. Check bit 1 of rs1 plus the immediate by evaluating its\n  low-bit expression separately for the three bypass candidates and\n  applying the registered rs1 masks to those checks. The existing full\n  address sum in EX/MEM supplies the aligned JALR target. A misaligned\n  control transfer suppresses its target request and link write, and a\n  misaligned boundary transfer still resumes fetch at PC+4. Qualify\n  each request once at EX advance, including dmem backpressure and the\n  multicycle wait on a non-control boundary instruction.\n\n  Register the three one-hot request enables in IF. Use their union to\n  squash younger ID work at the same edge, and select the corrected\n  fetch address on the following cycle. Keep the queue flush, stalled\n  request hold, last-word parking, predictor training schedule, and\n  single-channel retirement order. Maintain the ten-cycle liveness\n  bound for a divide reached through recovery.\n\n  A scratch precursor with the prepared enables and per-source JALR\n  alignment reached 86.566, 93.117, and 93.117 MHz and reproduced\n  3,956,104 timed cycles with canonical CRCs. It exposed fetch next-PC\n  feedback and the divider's final result path. The proposed staging of\n  PC-relative candidates tests whether the registered recovery packet\n  shortens the address handoff enough to improve the full design, with\n  the remaining EX paths as the principal risk. At unchanged cycles,\n  acceptance requires a median above 98.125 MHz. The impact estimate is\n  a target for the complete design. Repeat the three scored placements\n  and the unmodified formal, stalled cosim, and CoreMark validation.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \\\"Add the prepared recovery qualifiers and PC candidates to ID/EX, along with the displacement sign and halfword alignment bit.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Prepare the full wrapped sequential and B/J candidates and the legal recovery cases in parallel with operand selection. Capture them with the same ID/EX hold and validity rules.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Resolve the prepared cases into separate one-hot requests. Compute JALR alignment from the low bits of each forwarding candidate and retain the full effective address, branch training, link and RVFI successors.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Capture one-hot correction requests directly and use them to select the registered targets and their window flags. Preserve queue reset, address holding and parking semantics.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Wire the request enables separately into IF and combine them for the immediate younger-ID squash. Preserve the current memory and RVFI interfaces.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \\\"Exercise recovery for both branch outcomes, indirect targets from all bypass sources, blocked corrected requests, and a misaligned boundary JALR followed by a divide. Check exact retirement order and absence of wrong-path effects.\\\"\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nimport json\nimport jsonschema\nimport yaml\nfrom pathlib import Path\npath=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r10s1.yaml')\nh=yaml.safe_load(path.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(h, json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert h['id']==path.stem and h['category']=='structural'\nassert type(h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nassert all((Path('cores/bench')/entry['file']).is_file() for entry in h['changes'])\nprint('Schema, slot category, identifier, and change paths validated.')\nPY\ngit status --short\ndate -u\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
