{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-00[123]-r(9|10)s[012]' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; rows=[json.loads(l) for l in sys.stdin]; [print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"category\\\"],r.get(\\\"cycles\\\"),r.get(\\\"critical_path\\\"),\\\"\\\\n\\\",r.get(\\\"motivation\\\"),\\\"\\\\n\\\",r.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\")) for r in rows]'\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261121-001-r9s0 micro_opt 3956104 {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_sel_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/select_indirect_q_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.564, 'arrival_ns': 16.858, 'logic_cells': 37, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if']} \n The bench champion scores 225.49 CoreMark iterations per second at 89.207 MHz\nmedian Gowin Fmax. Its implementation notes identify the reciprocal seed ROM\nto first product path as the worst setup path in placements 1 and 2, which\nset the median. Removing the divider's per-update enables moves the setup\nlimit to the forwarded integer ALU. The ALU already forms the exact address\nsum for memory instructions and JALR, so a second full-width adder competes\nfor routing on the forwarded operands.\n \n # Shared EX address addition and divider staging\n\n- `alu.sv` exports the raw modulo-2^32 sum. The ALU ADD result and the EX/MEM effective-address register both use that sum. The existing EX/MEM stall holds the effective address and store payload together.\n- EX adds the low two bits of forwarded `rs1` and the immediate for JALR. Bit 1 controls the alignment trap, link eligibility, and indirect redirect. Fetch uses the registered full sum for the accepted, word-aligned target, and RVFI records the resolved successor.\n- The native divider updates its magnitude, sign, normalization, product, and correction registers every edge. The synchronous seed ROM is read every edge. The controller holds the launch operands through completion and qualifies the result after preparation and seven updates. The existing reset, consume, and ALTOPS timing still apply.\n- The ALU unit test also checks the raw sum for each vector, including vectors that select another ALU operation.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Strict Verilator lint and all seven cocotb suites passed. Divider vectors exercise held completion, reset cancellation, divide-by-zero, and signed overflow. Pipeline tests check the ten-cycle divide retirement bound.\n- Single-channel RVFI cosimulation passed for selftest and all \n…[9049 of 12121 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:226290c06833f754]…\n3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles without out-of-bounds accesses.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 90.031, 95.220, and 95.220 MHz. The 95.220 MHz median yields 240.69 CoreMark iterations/s, about 1.50% above the champion. The median is below the 98.125 MHz acceptance threshold. Forwarded ALU result selection and fetch-state feedback remain the limiting paths.\n\n\n hyp-20261121-003-r10s2 predictor 3559610 {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.effective_addr_9_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/even_valid_q_s1', 'slack_ns': -7.02, 'arrival_ns': 17.314, 'logic_cells': 14, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_if']} \n The bench champion scores 237.13 CoreMark iterations/sec at 93.81 MHz\nand 3,956,104 timed cycles. Backedge-scoped replay previously saved\nabout 7.7% of cycles. Tagging individual fetch predictions lets useful\nrecords survive exits and loop changes. A 512-entry scratch build with\none-word lookahead saved 11.12% of cycles with all five canonical CRCs.\nIts 84.015 MHz median was limited by a block-RAM-to-next-PC path.\n \n \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
