{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-(002-r9s1|002-r10s1|001-r10s0)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; [print(r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"].get(\\\"hypothesis\\\"),r[\\\"changes\\\"]) for r in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-002-r9s1 Replace the unified EX/MEM write-result register with three parallel\nregistered lanes for ADD/ADDI/SUB/AUIPC, other base integer writers,\nand JAL/JALR/RV32M. Decode the one-hot result-lane tag in ID, carry it\nwith the producer, and capture the lane results at the existing EX/MEM\nedge. For the add/subtract lane, use an ID-registered subtract bit to\ncomplement operand B and seed the carry for subtraction. Form carry-in\nzero and carry-in one candidates locally in each 8-bit block. Compute\ngroup carries in parallel from block propagate and generate signals,\nthen select each byte. Use the existing ALU operand choices for PC,\nimmediate and register inputs. This gives the sum a short path from\noperand forwarding to its own register, with modulo-2^32 wraparound.\n\nOn ID acceptance, resolve producer age first and register a one-hot\nmask selecting the future EX/MEM lane, the future MEM/WB write value,\nor the captured register-file value for each raw source. Form the masked\nEX/MEM lane terms in parallel with the MEM/WB and register-file terms\nand OR them in the EX operand mux. This keeps any ready EX result\navailable to the very next instruction. Keep the PC and immediate\noverrides in the registered ALU operand choices. In MEM, combine the\nmasked lanes and the formatted load as parallel sources of the existing\nMEM/WB write-value register. Loads still first forward from MEM/WB.\nA completed divide still commits from EX/MEM and supplies the same lane\nvalue for commit and forwarding.\n\nPreserve youngest-eligible priority, x0, the raw-source load-use interlock,\nand the alignment-qualified link and load producers. Freeze lane data,\ntags and consumer masks across dmem stalls and retain the held MEM/WB\nbypass value. Squashed and illegal instructions cannot supply a result.\nKeep RV32M and ALTOPS schedules, fetch and redirect behavior, and one\nordered RVFI retirement per cycle. At unchanged measured cycles, the\nacceptance threshold is above 93.31 MHz median Gowin Fmax. A median\naround 96.3 MHz would give roughly 8\n…[8552 of 11624 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:416d95d3b7ff19c2]…\nses and capture them with the same ID/EX hold and validity rules.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Resolve the prepared cases into separate one-hot requests. Compute JALR alignment from the low bits of each forwarding candidate and retain the full effective address, branch training, link and RVFI successors.'}, {'file': 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'description': 'Capture one-hot correction requests directly and use them to select the registered targets and their window flags. Preserve queue reset, address holding and parking semantics.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Wire the request enables separately into IF and combine them for the immediate younger-ID squash. Preserve the current memory and RVFI interfaces.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Exercise recovery for both branch outcomes, indirect targets from all bypass sources, blocked corrected requests, and a misaligned boundary JALR followed by a divide. Check exact retirement order and absence of wrong-path effects.'}]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
