{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_73", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,160p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r2s1.yaml && git status --short --untracked-files=all\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-002-r2s1\ntitle: Give the elastic fetch stage an early redirect path\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The iterative-divider champion scores 107.31 CoreMark iterations/sec at\n  48.196 MHz and 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles. Its fetch PC holds on\n  back-end stalls, and even a direct JAL waits for EX to redirect fetch.\n  The timed trace has 49,367 nonsequential JALs and 294,425 nonsequential\n  conditional branches. A two-entry fetch buffer alone reduced the timed\n  cycle count by 3.2 percent, which fell short of the acceptance threshold.\n  Giving the buffered front end an early path for transfers with a known\n  target and known operands can recover more of the idle fetch cycles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Put a two-entry fall-through FIFO of instruction words and full PCs\n  between the fetch PC and decode. When empty, let a ready fetch issue\n  directly to ID and capture it if ID is blocked. When nonempty, issue the\n  oldest word even if the instruction bus stalls. Advance the sequential\n  fetch PC on a ready bus whenever there is registered space. Allow a\n  simultaneous pop and refill with one buffered word. When full, allow a\n  pop and defer refill to the next cycle so fetch admission depends on\n  registered capacity. Reset empty. Keep sequential lookahead within the\n  instruction address window while older words are buffered.\n\n  On actual ID acceptance, steer the fetch PC to the aligned PC-relative\n  target of a JAL or of a taken BEQ or BNE whose source registers have no\n  pending nonzero writer in ID/EX or EX/MEM. Compare those branch operands\n  from the write-first register-file ports. Carry one bit with the issued\n  ID/EX instruction to suppress a duplicate EX redirect. Keep the issued\n  transfer in the pipeline and discard younger buffered words. The older\n  EX redirect wins over ID acceptance. For a valid, aligned transfer\n  resolved in EX, send its redirect once per EX entry even if an older\n  data access holds EX, then let the queue fill from the target while ID\n  waits. Hold both redirect tags \n…[1387 of 4459 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ab356ff43884c997]…\nwrite-first register file. Arbitrate older EX redirects first and track redirects already sent for the held or advancing EX entry.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Separate issue acceptance from fetch admission. Compare load-use dependencies against the valid FIFO head or bypass word, bubble without consuming it on a load-use match, and hold ID/EX for data or divider backpressure.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Capture the accepted FIFO head or bypass word and clear side-effect controls on invalid cycles and older EX redirects. Preserve the decoded payload during an ID/EX hold, including the PC of an early-steered transfer.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Check queue fill and ordered drain through both bus stalls, early JAL link forwarding, taken and untaken BEQ and BNE, EX fallback on a pending operand writer, and misaligned target traps. Include a redirect held by an older blocked store and verify one accepted store, exact RVFI PCs, and contiguous retirement order.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
