{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\"|hyp-20261121-001-r2s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261121-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV and REM to a shared iterative execute unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Bench's baseline closed at 5.442 MHz median Fmax and 12.12 CoreMark\\niterations per second. The reported 199.837 ns, 156-cell path runs from a\\nwriteback destination through forwarding and the EX ALU toward the data\\naddress path. The four combinational division and remainder expressions\\nfeed the same result mux as ordinary ALU operations and are the likely\\nsource of the deep logic. Every instruction pays their timing cost.\\nAn unmodified baseline cosim with both bus stalls enabled reproduced\\nthe logged 4,491,485 bracketed cycles and passed CoreMark CRC validation.\\nIts 2,922,015 timed retirements included zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU\\ninstructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Bench's baseline closed at 5.442 MHz median Fmax and 12.12 CoreMark\\niterations per second. The reported 199.837 ns, 156-cell path runs from a\\nwriteback destination through forwarding and the EX ALU toward the data\\naddress path. The four combinational division and remainder expressions\\nfeed the same result mux as ordinary ALU operations and are the likely\\nsource of the deep logic. Every instruction pays their timing cost.\\nAn unmodified baseline cosim with both bus stalls enabled reproduced\\nthe logged 4,491,485 bracketed cycles and passed CoreMark CRC validation.\\nIts 2,922,015 timed retirements included zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU\\ninstructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU into one blocking, clocked execute unit. At\\nlaunch, capture forwarded operands and the operation, and register the\\noperand magnitudes and sign flags. On the first update clock, compute\\nthe top radix-4 digit from the dividend magnitude's top two bits and\\nregister the divisor multiples D, 2D and 3D. Among nonzero divisors,\\nonly magnitudes one, two and three can yield a nonzero first digit. On\\neach of the next five clocks, cascade three radix-4 digit steps to\\nconsume the remaining 30 bits. A step a\n…[27824 of 30896 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f84a674e817d3ea8]…\nration validated.` All five CRCs matched (`0xe9f5`, `0xe714`, `0x1fd7`, `0x8e3a`, `0xfcaf`).\\n\\n## Local timing and fitness\\n\\nCoreMark took 4,195,317 cycles between the MMIO markers for 10 iterations. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 46.059, 48.391, and 48.391 MHz. Option 0 used 3,120 logic cells and 659 registers. The 48.391 MHz median gives 115.35 iterations per second, 7.49% above the 107.31 iterations per second champion. Option 0 falls below the hypothesis's timing threshold. Its worst path reaches data memory through EX forwarding and the ALU. The divider feedback path limits both higher-frequency placements.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-21T14:09:13.597710Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r2s1 (regression, +7.49%): The two-entry fetch FIFO with decode-stage JAL and operand-ready BEQ/BNE redirects cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.59%, with divider feedback capping median Gowin Fmax at 48.391 MHz against the 51.15 MHz needed to match the EX-tuned champion.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
