{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_60", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,180p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r3s1.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-002-r3s1\ntitle: \"Execute RV32M in a registered late-result lane\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The bench champion scores 121.92 iterations/s at 54.759 MHz and\n  4,491,485 timed CoreMark cycles. The forwarded low-word multiply is the\n  worst setup path in all three Gowin placements. Four cascaded restoring\n  divider steps are nearly tied in placements 1 and 2. The timed trace\n  contains 93,960 MULs and zero high multiplies, divides or remainders.\n  Giving RV32M a late-result lane can cut both setup paths while allowing\n  an independent instruction to follow a low multiply on the next cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Make EX/MEM the ordered issue slot for an RV32M lane beside data memory.\n  On a valid EX transfer, record the operation, destination and fully\n  forwarded operands. Take the result from this lane at the MEM/WB register,\n  as for a load. Leave ordinary ALU, address generation and branch resolution\n  in EX. Forward the registered late result from WB. Extend the existing\n  load-use interlock to both source-address fields behind an M producer.\n  The trace checker compares both raw fields even for instructions with an\n  immediate, so an alias in either field needs the correct pre-state value.\n  Exclude unfinished results from EX/MEM bypass. Low MUL completes in one\n  MEM cycle from registered operands, allowing one independent MUL per\n  cycle. High multiplies hold the slot for a second MEM cycle to register\n  the unsigned high product and signed correction before subtraction.\n\n  Launch DIV and REM on allocation of the same slot. Retain the existing\n  narrow four-bit launch seed. On the first iteration, use a narrow datapath\n  for the next four dividend bits, whose prefix is at most eight bits wide,\n  and register 3D from the captured divisor magnitude D in parallel. Use two\n  radix-4 digits per cycle for the six remaining four-bit groups. For each\n  digit, try D, 2D and 3D in parallel at full precision, choose the largest\n  multiple that fits, and append the digit. This keeps seven iteration\n \n…[3059 of 6131 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2a3dc04af0d5b33c]…\n    description: \"Extend the existing two-tag load interlock to late M producers. Let the same upstream hold policy handle a blocked bus access or a pending lane result.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Connect ordered allocation and completion. Mask late producers at the EX/MEM bypass input and route their registered WB values while preserving the existing ports and single-channel RVFI boundary.\"\n  - file: test/test_mul_unit.py\n    description: \"Retain exact RV32M multiply vectors and check consecutive low multiplies, high-result timing, and reset.\"\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: \"Retain exact RV32M divide and remainder checks and cover the eight-bit seed boundary, divisor multiples above 32 bits, and held completion.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Check independent and dependent multiplies, aliases in unused source fields, multiply-to-store and divide-to-branch forwarding, an older stalled memory request, and retirement order for mixed memory and M operations.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
