ycles and passed CoreMark CRC validation.\\nIts 2,922,015 timed retirements included zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU\\ninstructions.\\n\", 'hypothesis': {'motivation': \"Bench's baseline closed at 5.442 MHz median Fmax and 12.12 CoreMark\\niterations per second. The reported 199.837 ns, 156-cell path runs from a\\nwriteback destination through forwarding and the EX ALU toward the data\\naddress path. The four combinational division and remainder expressions\\nfeed the same result mux as ordinary ALU operations and are the likely\\nsource of the deep logic. Every instruction pay\n…[49918 of 52990 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f9ce4b59d7a2f68d]…\nms with zero-wait and stalled buses. CoreMark passed the canonical CRC, memory bounds, and timing marker checks with both stalls enabled.\\n- CoreMark took 4,190,307 marker-bracketed cycles for 10 iterations, a 6.71% cycle reduction from the champion. Gowin options 0, 1, and 2 measured 53.627, 58.220, and 58.220 MHz. The median yields 138.94 iterations/sec, 13.96% above 121.92 iterations/sec.\\n- Placement used 3,108 reported logic cells, 759 registers, and 12 LUT RAMs. The logic count is higher than the scratch prototype in the hypothesis. The worst setup paths still run through the EX ALU to data memory.\\n', 'timestamp': '2026-11-21T14:40:03.510960Z', 'slot': 2, 'round_id': 3, 'lesson': '- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r3s2 (improvement, +13.96%): Pairing a direction-only table with split PC-relative target arithmetic in IF avoided taken-transfer bubbles and let EX detect mispredictions with a one-bit comparison, cutting stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.71% while median Gowin Fmax rose from 54.759 to 58.220 MHz.'}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
