{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_101", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,96p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r6s1.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Retime execute operands and commit divides from EX/MEM\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The bench champion scores 168.92 iter/sec at 66.827 MHz median and 3,956,104 timed cycles. Its reported worst path runs from an ID/EX source address through the bypass and into the divider launch seed. Round 5 found that advancing bypass selection alone left divider setup or feedback on the critical path. In a scratch build, registering only the raw divide inputs exposed the main bypass-to-ALU and bypass-to-MUL paths at 66.321 MHz. Retime both ends of execute so ID selects the operands, divider launch captures them, and the divider's earlier architectural commit pays for the extra register cycle within the existing liveness window.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retiming the execute boundaries should preserve the five-entry fetch queue's cycle savings while shortening the paths from both bypass producer slots to execution. For each raw source field, compare in ID against the current EX and MEM destinations. Choose the youngest eligible nonzero writer and register a one-hot choice among the upcoming EX/MEM value, upcoming MEM/WB value, and captured register-file value. Qualify an EX link writer with its target alignment and a MEM load writer with its access alignment before choosing, so an older eligible match can supply the value if the younger instruction traps. For ALU inputs, register separate choices and preselect PC for AUIPC and the immediate for immediate operations. EX applies the registered masks to the three data candidates in parallel. Keep the load-use interlock on both raw fields, the write-first register-file read, and the selectors' ID/EX hold and flush semantics. Register one selected load or EX write value at MEM/WB and pr