{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_36", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'] in ('hyp-20261121-003-r6s2','hyp-20261121-003-r5s2'):\n  print(d['title'],d['critical_path'],d['seeds'])\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Decouple predicted fetch with a five-entry queue and registered recovery {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_addr_3_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/remainder_q_0_s1', 'slack_ns': -11.329, 'arrival_ns': 21.623, 'logic_cells': 17, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_div']} [61.241, 66.827, 66.827]\nFeed a five-entry circular fall-through queue from the champion's\n64-entry folded-PC direction predictor. Each slot is a 95-bit\nregister holding the 30-bit word PC, instruction, predicted-taken\nbit, and full 32-bit B/J target, including the halfword bit used\nfor traps. Retain the split PC-relative target sum in IF and carry\nthe selected prediction and target through the existing ID/EX\npayload. Give aligned in-window direct jumps their target on the\nfirst visit. Predict JALR sequentially.\n\nRotate one-hot read and write pointers independently and track\noccupancy with a five-bit unary count. Use a masked OR of the\nselected physical slot and bypass the array when empty. Pop only\nwhen decode admits the head. Fetch whenever imem is ready and\nthere is already a free slot, even if decode is held. A full queue\nfrees a slot on a pop and resumes fetching on the following cycle.\nThe fifth slot limits the cost of this policy while removing the\ncombinational decode-to-producer refill path. Write the selected\npayload slot on ordinary producer acceptance, including empty\nbypass, and advance its write pointer only on an actual enqueue.\nThat keeps decode admission off the wide payload write enables.\nAllow buffered issue while imem is unready.\n\nRead a mirrored counter high bit at the producer and read the\nfull two-bit counter at issue. Both copies use the same folded\nindex, reset-cleared validity and EX updates. Compute the issue\nindex from the selected instruction's PC. Keep the backward-taken\nand forward-sequential cold defaults. Forward a same-index update\ninto the issue-time counter snapshot. The pre-edge table selects\nthe fetch path, and EX trains each advancing nontrapping branch\nonce.\n\nKeep the one-bit direction mismat\n…[4700 of 7772 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1aa633fd58e1e113]…\nugh sequential recovery.\n\nThe scratch version took 3,650,094 marker-bracketed cycles with both\nstalls enabled, down 7.74%, and reproduced all five canonical CRCs.\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 59.228, 65.583, and\n65.583 MHz. The median gives 179.67 iter/sec, up 6.37% from 168.92.\nThis cycle count needs a median above 64.4936 MHz to clear the 4.6%\nthreshold. The divider launch path limits the median placement. Option\nzero used 4,292 reported logic cells, 6 block RAMs, and 2 DSPs.\nThe scratch RTL matched the ISS on selftest and the three random\nprograms with ready buses and with both stall generators enabled.\nAll seven cocotb suites passed with the three-slot request expectations\nand directed tests for replay over an unready imem request and a loop\npatched by a store followed by FENCE.I. All 53 single-channel ALTOPS\nformal checks passed, including forward-PC consistency and liveness.\nRepeat all three placements and the architectural checks after\nimplementation to measure the final timing margin.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
