pion.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r2s2 (regression, +8.73%): The forwarded ALU path limited the tagged BTB with parallel EX successor checks to 48.845 MHz median Gowin Fmax, below the 51.04 MHz needed to match the round’s EX-tuned winner after a 6.79% cut in stalled CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r3s0 \n…[16921 of 19993 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3449664a480f8d8f]…\n2.82 CoreMark iterations per second at 76.281 MHz\\nmedian Gowin Fmax and 3,956,104 timed cycles. Its reported divider feedback\\npath crosses two linked radix-4 steps. There are no timed DIV or REM\\nretirements, so the divider's feedback delay slows the benchmark without\\ndoing benchmark work. A scratch build that expanded the recurrence into\\n15 parallel trials fell to 71.803 MHz as trial logic and routing grew.\\nA feed-forward reciprocal pipeline maps most of the divide work into DSPs\\nand removes the wide remainder feedback path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the native recurrence with a fixed-schedule Goldschmidt divider\\nbuilt from a synchronous reciprocal seed ROM, registered multiply stages,\\nand an exact residual check. Keep the existing EX launch, eight update\\nedges, completion hold and consume protocol, and early EX/MEM retirement.\\nKeep the ALTOPS arithmetic and its matching schedule.\\n\\nAt preparation, capture operand magnitudes, zero detection, and the\\nquotient and remainder signs. On update 1, le\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
