{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r2s1','r3s1','r4s1','r6s2')):\n  print('##',d['id']); print(d['implementation_notes'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "## hyp-20261121-002-r2s1\n# Elastic fetch with early redirects\n\nFetch now has a two-entry fall-through FIFO that stores each instruction with its full PC. It admits ready fetch words using registered capacity, lets ID consume buffered words during instruction bus stalls, and refills on a one-entry pop. A full queue defers new admissions until the cycle after a pop. After buffering the final mapped instruction word, fetch holds its address until that word issues or a redirect clears the queue.\n\nAn issuing JAL or taken BEQ/BNE can redirect fetch from ID when its target is aligned. BEQ/BNE use the write-first register-file values when neither source has a pending nonzero writer in EX or MEM. The early target adder uses a B- or J-format offset. The issued instruction carries a bit into EX to suppress a second fetch redirect. EX computes the architectural next PC, link value, and misalignment trap, and handles the remaining transfers. A second bit in EX records a redirect sent while data backpressure holds that entry. EX redirects take priority over ID issue, and load-use bubbles leave the queued instruction available for the next cycle.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Strict Verilator lint passed in both hardware-arithmetic and ALTOPS modes.\n- The standard single-channel formal gate passed all 53 checks on the first run. It used the standard ALTOPS configuration and ready buses.\n- All 78 cocotb cases passed across six suites. Directed pipeline tests covered queue fill and ordered drain under both bus stalls, load-use interlocks, early JAL link forwarding, BEQ/BNE decisions and operand fallback, wrong-path discard, alignment traps, and the instruction-window boundary. A JALR held behind a blocked store redirected once, with one accepted store and exact RVFI PCs and order.\n- Co-simulation matched selftest and three random programs with ready buses and with both stall generators enabled. Stalled CoreMark retired EBREAK with no out-of-bounds access and printed `Correct operation validated.` All five CRCs matched (`0xe9f5`, `0xe\n…[8383 of 11455 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3e787d4342ff5184]…\nelds **182.82 iterations/s**, up **8.23%** from 168.92 and **2.236 MHz** above the frequency needed to clear the 4.6% fitness threshold.\n\nPlacement 0 reported 4,311 logic cells, 1,127 registers, 56 LUT RAMs, 6 block RAMs, and 2 DSPs. The longest reported setup path in each placement is from the predicted fetch PC through decode admission to last-word parking. The replay RAM write path is close in placements 1 and 2.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed in hardware and ALTOPS modes. All seven cocotb suites passed, covering 88 cases.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` matched the ISS on selftest and all three random programs with ready buses and with both stall generators enabled. Stalled CoreMark reported `Correct operation validated.` and matched all five canonical CRCs (`e9f5`, `e714`, `1fd7`, `8e3a`, `fcaf`).\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the retimed RTL, with zero failures or retries. This includes forward-PC consistency, register consistency, and liveness.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
