{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_76", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'nl -ba cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r8s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "     1\tid: hyp-20261121-002-r8s1\n     2\ttitle: Split speculative fetch from branch resolution and training\n     3\tcategory: structural\n     4\tmotivation: |\n     5\t  The bench champion scores 211.98 CoreMark iterations per second at\n     6\t  83.861 MHz median Gowin Fmax and 3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles.\n     7\t  Its placement-zero worst setup path runs from registered recovery\n     8\t  selection to fetch parking in 17.849 ns. The last-word hold depends\n     9\t  on target arithmetic, prediction, and decode admission. A nearly\n    10\t  tied path carries the forwarded EX branch outcome through counter\n    11\t  training and into ID/EX. Park on acceptance and give EX ownership of\n    12\t  full target resolution and the counter read for training. This\n    13\t  narrows the fetch queue payload and takes training out of decode.\n    14\thypothesis: |\n    15\t  Make IF own the outstanding request and a fall-through FIFO of ready\n    16\t  instruction words. Each entry carries the raw instruction, full word\n    17\t  PC, issued taken prediction, and a last-mapped-word marker. Remove\n    18\t  the full architectural direct target from the FIFO and ID/EX. Compute\n    19\t  the full RV32 B/J target in EX from the registered PC and immediate\n    20\t  alongside the branch comparison. Derive direct target misalignment\n    21\t  from immediate bit 1 so link eligibility stays off the target adder.\n    22\t  Feed the full EX result to the existing registered correction path\n    23\t  and use it for RVFI next-PC reporting. Keep the JALR recovery path.\n    24\t\n    25\t  The speculative fetch recurrence needs only an aligned target inside\n    26\t  the 1 MiB window. For a request inside that window, add the low 18\n    27\t  word-address bits and the low 18 displacement bits. Compute range\n    28\t  eligibility by comparing the adder carry with the signed displacement's\n    29\t  high bit. Use the zero-extended low target for an eligible\n    30\t  prediction. Carry the in-window and last-word bits alongside each\n    31\t  registered request candidat\n…[2977 of 6049 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:efa1351965421f22]…\n   description: \"Register the decoded B/J displacement, folded PC index, and ordered boundary marker with the instruction under the existing hold and flush controls.\"\n    74\t  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    75\t    description: \"Resolve the full 32-bit B/J target from registered inputs and trigger boundary recovery on EX advance. Send the valid branch outcome for training. Preserve alignment traps and the divide completion schedule.\"\n    76\t  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    77\t    description: \"Wire EX recovery into fetch and decode squash. Route the EX training index, offset sign, and valid branch outcome to the predictor while preserving the bus and single-channel RVFI ports.\"\n    78\t  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    79\t    description: \"Check mapped-edge acceptance with a full FIFO, a younger parked word squashed by an older redirect, ready and stalled corrected fetches, and a boundary JALR followed by a divide. Check exact RVFI PCs, order, link values, retirement gap, and consecutive training of aliased counters.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
