{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_64", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"for log in '/srv/hwebench/tmp/58928045/39c9f928/bench-predictor.kwJcyq'/bimodal_gowin/gowin_p*/gw.log; do tail -n 3 \\\"\"'$log\"; done; head -n 10 '\"'/srv/hwebench/tmp/58928045/39c9f928/bench-predictor.kwJcyq/bimodal_timing.log'; sed -n '1,130p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r1s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "[10%] Placement Phase 0 completed\n[20%] Placement Phase 1 completed\n[30%] Placement Phase 2 completed\n[10%] Placement Phase 0 completed\n[20%] Placement Phase 1 completed\n[30%] Placement Phase 2 completed\n[10%] Placement Phase 0 completed\n[20%] Placement Phase 1 completed\n[30%] Placement Phase 2 completed\nid: hyp-20261121-003-r1s2\ntitle: \"Predict hot control transfers with a single-read tagged target buffer\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The bench core fetches fall-through until EX resolves a control transfer. Each\n  aligned taken branch or jump normally discards one fetch opportunity because\n  IF feeds ID/EX without a separate IF/ID register. CoreMark repeatedly visits\n  loop branches, calls, and returns. The baseline's 5.442 MHz median Fmax is\n  limited by the forwarded ALU path, so the predictor must earn its gain in\n  marker-bracketed cycles while keeping the fetch feedback path short.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a 64-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer indexed by fetch PC[7:2],\n  with a full PC[31:8] tag, a word-aligned target, and a two-bit saturating\n  direction counter. A valid tag hit with the counter's high bit set selects\n  the stored target in parallel with the existing PC+4 calculation. Learn\n  taken conditional branches and JAL/JALR from EX, using the most recent\n  resolved target. Initialize a newly allocated conditional entry to weakly\n  taken and strengthen or weaken matching entries on outcomes. Install legal\n  aligned jumps as strongly taken. Train once per advancing valid EX control\n  instruction and suppress training for traps. Reset the valid bits and leave\n  the payload arrays unreset to allow distributed RAM inference.\n\n  Use only the fetch-indexed table read. On an accepted fetch, register that\n  instruction's lookup hit and counter alongside the IF PC update for use by\n  EX training one stage later. If EX writes the same index on that edge,\n  capture the new counter and determine the captured hit by comparing the\n  writing PC tag with the fetch PC tag. Use this bypass only for training\n…[1975 of 5047 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f2369ea66ef30b1d]…\nution and retain the existing branch and\n      jump alignment and link semantics.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: >-\n      Wire EX resolution to the predictor and generate recovery by comparing\n      the resolved next PC against the current fetch PC. Gate recovery and\n      training with valid EX advancement and keep the single-channel RVFI ports.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: >-\n      Keep the dmem hold predicate independent of recovery, give the hold\n      priority over bubbles, and retain imem and load-use gating on fetch\n      acceptance. Flush younger work on a next-PC mismatch.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: >-\n      Check that a trained taken branch admits its target without a bubble\n      when both buses are ready. Test the loop exit and a warmed indirect jump\n      whose target changes when an older store blocks EX and imem is stalled at\n      recovery. Check the retirement PC sequence and side-effect sentinels for\n      wrong-path stores and register writes.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
