{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_40", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,115p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r2s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-003-r2s2\ntitle: Predict hot transfers with a tagged BTB and parallel recovery checks\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current bench champion loses one fetch slot on each aligned taken\n  branch or jump because IF feeds ID/EX directly and resolves transfers in EX.\n  The round-one 64-entry BTB reduced marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles from\n  4,491,485 to 4,186,330 with both bus stalls enabled. Its Fmax was measured\n  on the older combinational-divider core. The iterative-divider champion\n  reaches 48.196 MHz, and the other divider trial exposed an EX-to-fetch\n  timing path. Preserving that clock while removing taken-transfer bubbles\n  is the opportunity for this predictor.\nhypothesis: |\n  On the current iterative-divider champion, use a 64-entry direct-mapped\n  BTB with fetch PC[7:2] indexing, full PC[31:8] tags, aligned targets, and\n  two-bit direction counters. Read once per fetch in parallel with PC+4.\n  Select the stored target on a valid, matching, predicted-taken entry.\n  Allocate taken conditional misses weakly taken and saturate matching\n  entries toward each resolved outcome. Preserve the target on not-taken\n  updates. Install aligned JAL and JALR as strongly taken, with the last\n  resolved target for JALR. Train only on advancing valid, nontrapping EX\n  control instructions. Reset the valid bits and leave payload RAM unreset.\n  Capture hit and counter with each accepted fetch. Bypass a concurrent\n  same-index write into this training snapshot using the written tag to\n  determine hit. The pre-edge lookup determines the fetch successor.\n\n  Use the current fetch PC as the successor previously selected for the\n  valid ID/EX instruction. Capture PC+4 with ID/EX using the existing IF\n  incrementer. In EX, compare that successor independently with PC+4, the\n  direct PC-relative target, and the aligned JALR target. Select the match\n  using the resolved outcome and existing alignment guards. Compute these\n  comparisons in parallel with branch direction and next-PC selection to\n  avoid seriali\n…[1952 of 5024 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:aa528313319e822d]…\n+4 under the same hold, flush, and admission conditions as the decoded instruction.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Compare the current fetch PC separately against sequential, direct, and JALR successors in parallel with outcome resolution. Export mismatch, actual successor, and valid control outcomes while preserving trap, link, divider, and RVFI behavior.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect predictor training to valid EX advancement and route the resolved mismatch to fetch correction and the younger-instruction squash.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Keep the dmem hold independent of recovery and give it priority over a younger squash. Evaluate load-use against the raw fetch word and inject ID/EX bubbles for load-use, imem stalls, and recovery when EX advances.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Check bubble-free trained branches, loop-exit and changing-JALR correction across bus stalls, and stale-entry recovery with no wrong-path stores or register writes.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
