outcomes. Install legal\\naligned jumps as strongly taken. Train once per advancing valid EX control\\ninstruction and suppress training for traps. Reset the valid bits and leave\\nthe payload arrays unreset to allow distributed RAM inference.\\n\\nUse only the fetch-indexed table read. On an accepted fetch, register that\\ninstruction's lookup hit and counter alongside the IF PC update for use by\\nEX training on\n…[31908 of 34980 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7323d1b7efcdd905]…\nero-wait and stalled buses. CoreMark passed the canonical CRC, memory bounds, and timing marker checks with both stalls enabled.\\n- CoreMark took 4,190,307 marker-bracketed cycles for 10 iterations, a 6.71% cycle reduction from the champion. Gowin options 0, 1, and 2 measured 53.627, 58.220, and 58.220 MHz. The median yields 138.94 iterations/sec, 13.96% above 121.92 iterations/sec.\\n- Placement used 3,108 reported logic cells, 759 registers, and 12 LUT RAMs. The logic count is higher than the scratch prototype in the hypothesis. The worst setup paths still run through the EX ALU to data memory.\\n\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-21T14:40:03.510960Z\",\n  \"slot\": 2,\n  \"round_id\": 3,\n  \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r3s2 (improvement, +13.96%): Pairing a direction-only table with split PC-relative target arithmetic in IF avoided taken-transfer bubbles and let EX detect mispredictions with a one-bit comparison, cutting stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.71% while median Gowin Fmax rose from 54.759 to 58.220 MHz.\"\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
