{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_18", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat fpga/core_bench_si.sv && sed -n '1,275p' tools/eval/gowin.py\"", "aggregated_output": "// fpga/core_bench_si.sv\n//\n// FPGA Fmax wrapper for single-issue (`nret 1`) cores. Same shape as\n// core_bench.sv but instantiates `core` with only the channel-0 RVFI port\n// set — no `_1` ports. Selected by the orchestrator (via BENCH env var in\n// synth.tcl) when cores/<target>/core.yaml declares nret: 1.\n//\n// See core_bench.sv for the rationale on LFSR-driven imem, the dmem\n// model, the V2 stall generator, and why the LED observes only the\n// memory-side outputs (RVFI-only logic is pruned).\nmodule core_bench (\n  input  logic clock,\n  input  logic reset,\n  output logic led\n);\n\n  logic [31:0] lfsr;\n  always_ff @(posedge clock or posedge reset)\n    if (reset) lfsr <= 32'h1;\n    else       lfsr <= {lfsr[30:0], lfsr[31] ^ lfsr[21] ^ lfsr[1] ^ lfsr[0]};\n\n  logic [31:0] dmem [0:2047];\n  logic [31:0] dmem_rdata;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_addr;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_wdata;\n  logic [3:0]  dmem_wen;\n  logic        dmem_ren;\n\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if (dmem_wen[0]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][7:0]   <= dmem_wdata[7:0];\n    if (dmem_wen[1]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][15:8]  <= dmem_wdata[15:8];\n    if (dmem_wen[2]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][23:16] <= dmem_wdata[23:16];\n    if (dmem_wen[3]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][31:24] <= dmem_wdata[31:24];\n  end\n  assign dmem_rdata = dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]];\n\n  logic [31:0] imem_addr;\n  // Single-channel RVFI port set (NRET=1 contract). Connected for\n  // elaboration only; nothing observes it, so synthesis prunes it.\n  logic        rvfi_valid_0;\n  logic [63:0] rvfi_order_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_insn_0, rvfi_pc_rdata_0, rvfi_pc_wdata_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_rd_wdata_0, rvfi_rs1_rdata_0, rvfi_rs2_rdata_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_mem_addr_0, rvfi_mem_rdata_0, rvfi_mem_wdata_0;\n  logic [4:0]  rvfi_rs1_addr_0, rvfi_rs2_addr_0, rvfi_rd_addr_0;\n  logic [3:0]  rvfi_mem_rmask_0, rvfi_mem_wmask_0;\n  logic [1:0]  rvfi_mode_0, rvfi_ixl_0;\n  logic        rvfi_trap_0, rvfi_halt_0, rvfi_intr_0;\n\n  // V2: memory-ready signals come from the same stall sequence the\n  // CoreMark simulation uses, so stall-handling logic\n…[11230 of 14302 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:efda22e048e111f8]…\n:\n            print(f\"place_option {r['place_option']}: FAILED ({r.get('reason')})\")\n            continue\n        print(f\"place_option {r['place_option']}: Fmax {r['fmax_mhz']} MHz, {r['levels']} logic levels; \"\n              f\"logic {r['logic']}, registers {r['regs']}, LUT-RAM {r['lutram']}, BSRAM {r['bsram']}, DSP {r['dsp']}\")\n        tr = (wt / \"cores\" / a.target / \"generated\" / \"timing\" / f\"gowin_p{r['place_option']}\" / \"impl/pnr/core_bench.tr\")\n        for i, p in enumerate(critical_paths(tr.read_text(errors=\"replace\"), a.paths), 1):\n            print(f\"  path {i}: {p['arrival_ns']} ns, {p['logic_cells']} cells, {p['from']} -> {p['to']}\")\n            print(f\"          through: {' > '.join(p['modules'])}\")\n        print(f\"  full report: {tr}\")\n    if len(res) > 1 and all(not r[\"placement_failed\"] for r in res):\n        print(f\"score Fmax (median): {summarize(res)['fmax_mhz']} MHz\")\n    return 0 if all(not r[\"placement_failed\"] for r in res) else 1\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-(001|002)-r3s[01]\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; [print(x[\\\"id\\\"], x[\\\"fitness\\\"], x[\\\"cycles\\\"], x[\\\"fmax_mhz\\\"], x[\\\"critical_path\\\"], x[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"],x[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\") for x in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_19", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-(001|002)-r3s[01]\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; [print(x[\\\"id\\\"], x[\\\"fitness\\\"], x[\\\"cycles\\\"], x[\\\"fmax_mhz\\\"], x[\\\"critical_path\\\"], x[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"],x[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\") for x in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r3s0\n133.91\n4491485\n60.145\n{'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/remainder_q_3_s1', 'slack_ns': -11.905, 'arrival_ns': 22.199, 'logic_cells': 16, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_div']}\nGive the EX/MEM register a dedicated effective-address field fed by\nforwarded rs1 plus the decoded immediate. Drive the LSU address,\nalignment checks, lane masks and load extraction from that field. This\nremoves the ALU and multiplier result mux from the memory address path\nwhile keeping MUL single-cycle. Hold the address and store payload together\nunder data bus backpressure.\n\nShorten the remaining divider feedback while preserving its eight wait\ncycles. Keep the four-bit launch seed. On the first iteration, process\nthe next four bits in a narrow eight-bit restoring datapath while\nregistering three times the unsigned divisor magnitude in parallel.\nA divisor of at least 256 cannot take any of those four quotient bits.\nFor the remaining six iterations use two radix-4 steps per cycle. Each\nstep tries the widened subtractions of D, 2D and 3D in parallel and\nselects the remainder from the largest multiple that fits. Use a 34-bit\nshifted remainder and divisor multiples with a 35-bit subtraction to keep\nthe borrow. This cuts the wide subtract-and-select chain from four to two.\n\nThe scratch implementation measured 63.182, 60.868 and 60.868 MHz across\nGowin placements. Stalled CoreMark validated all five canonical CRCs in\n4,491,485 timed cycles, giving 135.52 iter/s, up 11.15%. Placement 0 used\n3,022 logic cells and 726 registers. The prototype passed 53 single-channel\nALTOPS checks, all seven cocotb runners, and selftest plus three random\nRVFI traces with ready and stalled buses. Directed checks passed for the\n255/256 divisor boundary and a stalled byte store with a forwarded base.\n\n\nhyp-20261121-002-r3s1\n138.83\n4527760\n62.861\n{'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0', 'to': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -10.748, 'arrival_ns': 21.043, 'logic_cells': 18, 'modules': ['clock\n…[4847 of 7919 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:032529fca8a67e18]…\nraw RVFI source aliases, a stalled older store, and a divide at a redirect target.\n- Co-simulation matched selftest and three random programs against the ISS in both zero-wait and stalled modes. Stalled CoreMark produced all five canonical CRCs and reported `Correct operation validated.`\n\n## CoreMark and Gowin\n\n| Gowin placement | Fmax |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 63.498 MHz |\n| 1 | 62.861 MHz |\n| 2 | 62.861 MHz |\n| Median | 62.861 MHz |\n\nThe 10-iteration run took 4,527,760 bracketed cycles, up 0.81% from the champion. The measured fitness was **138.83 iterations/s**, up 13.87%. Every placement exceeded the stated 57.74 MHz bar. Placement 0 used 2,961 logic cells, 667 registers, and two DSPs. The worst setup paths crossed the integer ALU into the data-address path.\n\nAn immediate dependency on a low multiply adds one issue bubble, including a dependency through a raw RVFI source field. High multiply adds one MEM wait cycle. Divide and remainder retain eight extra MEM cycles and hold younger issue during that time.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-002-r2s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; x=json.load(sys.stdin); print(x[\\\"category\\\"],x[\\\"cycles\\\"],x[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"],x[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")' && rg 'idx= 8 hist= 8' \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/predictor-profile/analyze.txt\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_20", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-002-r2s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; x=json.load(sys.stdin); print(x[\\\"category\\\"],x[\\\"cycles\\\"],x[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"],x[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")' && rg 'idx= 8 hist= 8' \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/predictor-profile/analyze.txt\"'", "aggregated_output": "structural\n4195317\nPut a two-entry fall-through FIFO of instruction words and full PCs\nbetween the fetch PC and decode. When empty, let a ready fetch issue\ndirectly to ID and capture it if ID is blocked. When nonempty, issue the\noldest word even if the instruction bus stalls. Advance the sequential\nfetch PC on a ready bus whenever there is registered space. Allow a\nsimultaneous pop and refill with one buffered word. When full, allow a\npop and defer refill to the next cycle so fetch admission depends on\nregistered capacity. Reset empty. Keep sequential lookahead within the\ninstruction address window while older words are buffered.\n\nOn actual ID acceptance, steer the fetch PC to the aligned PC-relative\ntarget of a JAL or of a taken BEQ or BNE whose source registers have no\npending nonzero writer in ID/EX or EX/MEM. Compare those branch operands\nfrom the write-first register-file ports. Carry one bit with the issued\nID/EX instruction to suppress a duplicate EX redirect. Keep the issued\ntransfer in the pipeline and discard younger buffered words. The older\nEX redirect wins over ID acceptance. For a valid, aligned transfer\nresolved in EX, send its redirect once per EX entry even if an older\ndata access holds EX, then let the queue fill from the target while ID\nwaits. Hold both redirect tags with the EX entry and clear them on\nreset or when that entry leaves. EX still computes the architectural\nnext PC, link value and alignment trap. Other branches and JALR keep\nEX resolution. Load-use interlocks consume no queued word, and the\nexisting data-bus handshake and single-channel RVFI order apply.\n\nA temporary prototype with this structure ran the canonical stalled\nCoreMark in 4,195,317 marker-bracketed cycles with all five expected\nCRCs, EBREAK, and no out-of-bounds access. Gowin placement options 0,\n1, and 2 reached 47.750, 48.364, and 48.364 MHz. Option 0 used 3,176\nlogic cells and 658 registers. The median implies 115.28 iterations/sec,\nabout 7.4 percent above the champion. At that cycle count the\nimplementation needs ab\n…[2057 of 5129 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:cefe5edcf0711f97]…\nd order.\n- Co-simulation matched selftest and three random programs with ready buses and with both stall generators enabled. Stalled CoreMark retired EBREAK with no out-of-bounds access and printed `Correct operation validated.` All five CRCs matched (`0xe9f5`, `0xe714`, `0x1fd7`, `0x8e3a`, `0xfcaf`).\n\n## Local timing and fitness\n\nCoreMark took 4,195,317 cycles between the MMIO markers for 10 iterations. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 46.059, 48.391, and 48.391 MHz. Option 0 used 3,120 logic cells and 659 registers. The 48.391 MHz median gives 115.35 iterations per second, 7.49% above the 107.31 iterations per second champion. Option 0 falls below the hypothesis's timing threshold. Its worst path reaches data memory through EX forwarding and the ALU. The divider feedback path limits both higher-frequency placements.\n\nmiss   60200 ( 5243 adjacent) idx= 8 hist= 8 resolved-global xor\nmiss   57221 ( 4483 adjacent) idx= 8 hist= 8 spec-global xor\nmiss   45503 ( 5280 adjacent) idx= 8 hist= 8 local xor\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"The champion trace leaves little room for direction accuracy alone. Even correcting every conditional miss and return would save only about 55,000 recovery slots before stall overlap. I’ll test whether the existing predictor can fetch ahead during decode stalls and keep decode fed during instruction-bus stalls."}}
