{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(s)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261121-003-r6s2','hyp-20261121-003-r7s2','hyp-20261121-002-r7s1','hyp-20261121-001-r7s0']:\n  for k,v in d.items():\n   if k in ['id','title','cycles','fitness','fmax_mhz','hypothesis','lut4','implementation_notes']:\n    print(k, v)\nPY\nrg --files -g '*coremark*' -g '*timing*'; git branch --show-current; cat cores/bench/CORE_PHILOSOPHY.md\"", "aggregated_output": "id hyp-20261121-003-r6s2\ntitle Replay short instruction loops selected by resolved backedges\nhypothesis {'motivation': 'The champion uses 3,956,104 timed cycles at 66.827 MHz. Its fetch queue\\nabsorbs stalls while it has buffered words, but every new instruction\\nstill requires a ready imem cycle. The round-four trace put conditional\\ndirection misses and JALRs together at roughly 56,000 events, so a\\ndirection or return accuracy improvement alone offers limited headroom.\\nReusing instructions on predicted loop iterations can also hide\\ninstruction bus stalls. A scratch run with the champion and only dmem\\nstalls took 3,371,582 cycles, showing the headroom in instruction supply.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Attach a loop stream buffer to the predicted fetch producer. Train one\\nloop descriptor from an advancing, aligned, taken backward conditional\\nbranch whose inclusive body spans at most 128 words in the 1 MiB\\ninstruction window. Register the event separately from the unconditionally\\ncaptured EX target and branch PC so the branch condition only drives\\nthe training-valid bit. Keep the current descriptor on an inner\\nbackedge fully contained in its range. Stage a refill when a ready bus word\\nmatches the producer PC, then write only aligned words in the tracked\\nloop that precede the last mapped word. Clear word validity on\\nallocation, so the seven low word-PC bits uniquely index the current\\nloop. Qualify a replay with the full upper address, the stored page bit\\nthat distinguishes two adjacent 512-byte pages, and per-word validity.\\n\\nStore each instruction, page bit, and the precomputed 18-bit word\\ntarget and target eligibility bit in synchronous block RAM. Read the\\nRAM at the computed next producer index on the same edge that captures\\nthe next producer PC. Treat a same-index read/write collision as a\\nmiss for the following cycle. Use the existing folded direction table\\nfor both sources and select between the bus target, the replay target\\nand fall-through in parallel. Compute the bus target with a 19-bit\\nword\n…[29187 of 32259 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a288c298c96cf633]…\nst `e714`, matrix `1fd7`, state `8e3a`, final\n  `fcaf`, and “Correct operation validated.” No out-of-bounds access was\n  reported.\n\n## Gowin and CoreMark\n\n| Placement option | Fmax |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 70.738 MHz |\n| 1 | 75.282 MHz |\n| 2 | 75.282 MHz |\n| Median | 75.282 MHz |\n\nTen CoreMark iterations took **3,651,216** marker-bracketed cycles,\n7.71% fewer than the champion's 3,956,104 and 1,122 more than the prior\nreplay trial. The measured score is **206.18 iterations/sec**, 6.93%\nabove 192.82 and above the 4.6% acceptance margin. Placement zero used\n4,504 logic cells, 1,439 registers, 40 LUT-RAM cells, and six BSRAMs.\nThe divider recurrence is the worst path in all three placements.\nPlacement zero also has a close path from registered recovery selection\nto the replay RAM read address.\n\nbench/programs/coremark/coremark.h\nbench/programs/coremark/coremark.md5\nbench/programs/coremark/docs/coremark_profile_o0_joined.png\nbench/programs/coremark/docs/html/files/coremark-h.html\nbench-hyp-20261121-003-r8s2-hypgen\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
