{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(s)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261121-003-r3s2','hyp-20261121-003-r4s2','hyp-20261121-003-r5s2']:\n  print(d['id'], d['hypothesis'], d['implementation_notes'], sep='\\\\n')\nPY\nls -a /srv/hwebench/toolchain; git branch --list '*r7s2*'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r3s2\n{'motivation': 'The current champion runs ten timed CoreMark iterations in 4,491,485 cycles\\nat 54.759 MHz. The tagged BTB trial on the round-two starting RTL saved\\n6.79% of cycles and scored 116.68 iter/sec at a 48.845 MHz median. The\\ncurrent champion scores 121.92 iter/sec. The bench pipeline has no IF/ID\\nregister, so predicting a direct transfer at fetch acceptance can still put\\nthe target in front of EX on the next cycle. Deriving that target from the\\nfetched instruction lets EX verify direction with one bit and removes the\\ntarget RAM and full-address match network used in the prior predictor.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Add a 64-entry table of two-bit conditional-branch direction counters.\\nIndex it with PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8] at fetch and training to spread branches\\nwith the same low PC bits across entries. Reset only the entry-valid bits\\nso the counters can map to distributed RAM. Start unused entries at weakly\\ntaken for backward branches and weakly sequential for forward branches.\\nPredict using the counter's high bit and saturate toward the resolved\\ndirection. Predict every aligned JAL to its direct target on the first\\nvisit. Resolve JALR in EX through the existing forwarded operand path. The\\ninstruction determines whether a prediction applies and supplies its target,\\nso table aliasing changes only the direction of a conditional branch.\\n\\nPredecode the B/J opcodes in IF, including the six legal branch funct3 values,\\nand form the PC-relative target for the current instruction alongside PC+4.\\nUse word-address arithmetic with the low target bits carried separately for\\nalignment. Add the low 19 word-address bits to the sign-extended 19-bit\\nword offset. Precompute the unchanged, incremented and decremented values\\nof PC[31:21] in parallel. Select them by applying the low carry minus the\\noffset sign to the high part. Use this full computed target in EX for\\narchitectural resolution.\\n\\nQualify taken conditional predictions with a zero check of target[31:20]\\nso speculative branch \n…[20360 of 23432 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:61fa3f036a221369]…\nncluding EX/MEM effective address with bit zero cleared for JALR. Drive the existing ID/EX validity squash from the live EX mismatch.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Export EX advance, the actual direct and indirect transfer selects, PC+4, and the latched direct target. Keep the actual-next-PC mux local to RVFI, preserve the one-bit mismatch check and train only on valid nontrapping branch advancement.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Hold an older store long enough to fill five slots, check that the full producer can resume on the cycle after a pop, and check buffered issue while imem is unready. Update the loop-exit request trace to show runahead, recovery over an unready wrong-path fetch, and a held first corrected request. Exercise JALR behind a stalled store and JALR at the last mapped word. Check links, retirement order, and wrong-path side-effect sentinels and retain the same-index training and trap cases.'}]}\n\n.\n..\nbin\noss-cad-suite\nxpack-riscv-none-elf-gcc-15.2.0-1\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
