{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r.get('id','').endswith(('r8s1','r8s2','r7s2')):\n  print(r['id'],{k:r[k] for k in ('title','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','critical_path','error','outcome')})\n  print(r.get('implementation_notes','')[:9500])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r7s2 {'title': 'Replay predicted loops with fetch-side boundary handling', 'fitness': 206.18, 'fmax_mhz': 75.282, 'cycles': 3651216, 'lut4': 4504, 'ff': 1439, 'critical_path': {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/remainder_q_0_s1', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/remainder_q_0_s1', 'slack_ns': -9.137, 'arrival_ns': 19.431, 'logic_cells': 32, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_div']}, 'error': None, 'outcome': 'regression'}\n# Fetch-side loop replay\n\nFetch tracks one loop range of at most 128 words. A staged, advancing,\naligned conditional backedge can replace the range. A contained backedge\nkeeps it. Accepted bus words in the range stage a RAM write with the\ninstruction, direct target, eligibility, and low page bit. The synchronous\nread follows the selected next producer PC. The lookup checks the full\npage address, the saved page bit, validity, and the registered write index.\nThe folded direction predictor serves both instruction sources. Its\nrecovery candidates are folded in parallel before selection to shorten\nthe corrected-fetch path.\n\nThe three-entry queue uses the champion's one-hot ring of register slots.\nA matching ready bus response takes priority. When replay advances over\nan unready external request, fetch holds its address and discards the\nresponse on readiness. Registered EX recovery supersedes the hold and\ncan refill the emptied queue on its corrected cycle.\n\nFetch marks the ready word at `0x000ffffc` on ordinary acceptance and\nholds the producer there. When the marker advances in EX, recovery uses\nthe architectural successor. An older redirect discards a younger\nmarker. EX reconstructs the full direct target from the registered PC\nand instruction and obtains the alignment bit from the immediate.\nAccepted stores that overlap the tracked range clear word validity.\nAdvancing FENCE.I clears the descriptor and queued refills and recovers\nto the sequential PC. The operand forwarding and divide schedules and\nthe single-channel retirement interface are preserved.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed\n…[7928 of 11000 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3582dd04c07eab48]…\ns, and the illegal-instruction check. `reg_ch0` remained in progress at handoff with no counterexample reported.\n\nThe formal and co-simulation commands use scratch copies of the existing harnesses and benchmark inputs. The formal wrapper ties both readiness inputs high. The directed tests and stalled co-simulation exercise replay with changing readiness.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\n| Placement | Fmax (MHz) | Worst setup path |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| 0 | 79.285 | Direct replay block RAM to the ID/EX operand through the register file |\n| 1 | 75.288 | Registered direct recovery select to predicted fetch PC |\n| 2 | 75.288 | Registered direct recovery select to predicted fetch PC |\n\nThe median was 75.288 MHz. At 3,648,511 bracketed cycles, that yields 206.35 iterations/sec, 2.65% below the champion. The 4.6% acceptance margin requires 80.90 MHz at this cycle count. The selected implementation had the highest median of the locally measured variants. It used 5,170 logic cells, 2,006 registers, nine block RAMs and nine DSPs.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
