{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json; keys=\\\"id title cycles fmax_mhz fitness delta_pct\\\".split(); [(print(*(str(r[k]) for k in keys),sep=\\\" | \\\"),print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"])) for r in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r1s2 | Predict hot control transfers with a single-read tagged target buffer | 4186330 | 5.533 | 13.22 | 9.08\n{'motivation': \"The bench core fetches fall-through until EX resolves a control transfer. Each\\naligned taken branch or jump normally discards one fetch opportunity because\\nIF feeds ID/EX without a separate IF/ID register. CoreMark repeatedly visits\\nloop branches, calls, and returns. The baseline's 5.442 MHz median Fmax is\\nlimited by the forwarded ALU path, so the predictor must earn its gain in\\nmarker-bracketed cycles while keeping the fetch feedback path short.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Add a 64-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer indexed by fetch PC[7:2],\\nwith a full PC[31:8] tag, a word-aligned target, and a two-bit saturating\\ndirection counter. A valid tag hit with the counter's high bit set selects\\nthe stored target in parallel with the existing PC+4 calculation. Learn\\ntaken conditional branches and JAL/JALR from EX, using the most recent\\nresolved target. Initialize a newly allocated conditional entry to weakly\\ntaken and strengthen or weaken matching entries on outcomes. Install legal\\naligned jumps as strongly taken. Train once per advancing valid EX control\\ninstruction and suppress training for traps. Reset the valid bits and leave\\nthe payload arrays unreset to allow distributed RAM inference.\\n\\nUse only the fetch-indexed table read. On an accepted fetch, register that\\ninstruction's lookup hit and counter alongside the IF PC update for use by\\nEX training one stage later. If EX writes the same index on that edge,\\ncapture the new counter and determine the captured hit by comparing the\\nwriting PC tag with the fetch PC tag. Use this bypass only for training\\nmetadata. The next-PC prediction uses the pre-edge lookup. Hold the captured\\nmetadata during dmem backpressure so it stays paired with ID/EX.\\n\\nKeep EX authoritative and compare its resolved next PC with the current\\nfetch PC, which is the successor selected when that instruction entered\\nID/EX. Check every advanc\n…[41177 of 44249 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:bfff798bf90cf6f9]…\ntion': \"Carry a last-mapped-word marker alongside each instruction's existing target and predictor metadata.\"}, {'file': 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'description': 'Carry the boundary marker through the existing ID/EX capture, hold and validity squash.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Qualify aligned taken backedge training on EX advance and use registered recovery for an advancing boundary instruction or FENCE.I. Retain the existing predictor verification and resolved RVFI successor.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Wire the advancing backedge information, FENCE.I event, and accepted store word address from the existing EX and dmem interfaces to fetch.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Check sequential and taken replay, two-page ranges, a held replay result, collision fallback, and older recovery over an outstanding request. Check boundary admission with a full queue and warmed replay invalidation after a store and FENCE.I, including ordered PCs and memory side effects.'}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
