{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_44", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(l) for l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()]\nfor r in rows:\n if any(x in r['id'] for x in ('r13s0','r13s1','r10s0','r11s1')):\n  print('\\\\n',r['id'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],r['implementation_notes'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261121-001-r10s0 Allow a legal BEQ or BNE to issue directly behind an aligned load it\ndepends on. Keep the bypass local to the branch equality comparator.\nCompare the raw memory word with the other forwarded operand in parallel\nby access width. For a byte or halfword, check the other operand's upper\nbits against the required sign or zero extension of its own low bits.\nIn parallel, compare those low bits with the raw memory lanes, then use\nthe registered load width and address to select the comparison. A word\nload compares all 32 bits directly. If both sources select the same load,\nequality is true. Preserve the formatted load path for writeback and\nRVFI. Keep the existing interlock for every other load-use pair.\n\nPrecompute the sequential and direct correction enables for each\npossible equality outcome from the registered branch type, prediction,\nalignment and boundary flags. Select the enables with the load\ncomparison and capture them in the existing IF recovery registers when\nthe load is accepted. Use the registered correction to suppress all\neffects of the speculatively captured younger EX instruction while ID\naccepts the corrected fetch. Gate off younger bypass eligibility and\nMUL/DIV launch on that edge. Keep ordinary EX recovery on its existing\nschedule. Capture the equality outcome for the late branch's ordered RVFI\nretirement and report the actual loaded source values.\n\nKeep predictor RAM lookup and saturating counter arithmetic off the\nload-response path. Register the incremented and decremented counter\ncandidates in parallel with the branch outcome, then select the pending\nupdate with the registered outcome and forward that same value on an\nindex alias. Qualify training with the registered alignment so a trapped\ntaken branch cannot update the table. Keep the predictor policy, memory\nhandshake and single-channel retirement contract. At approximately\n3.845 million cycles, the required median Gowin Fmax is above 95.4 MHz.\nA median of 96.7 MHz would yield about 6% higher fitness. The raw-lane\ncompar\n…[17401 of 20473 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1763d0bd4a767eba]…\n stalled RVFI\ncosim for selftest and the three random programs. The fixed head lifted\nplacement 1 from 83.447 to 89.072 MHz and moved the limit to ALU feedback.\nThat prototype placed at 88.616, 89.072, and 89.072 MHz, scoring 262.92\niterations/sec. It also delayed ordinary branch training by an edge,\nwhich broke the existing corrected-fetch predictor test. Restoring\nimmediate normal training passed the pipeline suite including the\ncorrected-fetch assertion and new chained-load and squash tests, and\nreproduced the same CoreMark cycle count. That version reached 81.803\nMHz on placement 1, limited by EX operand forwarding into training.\nThe parallel counter candidates and post-edge qualification are the\nproposed timing cut for that path.\nAt the measured cycle count the median must exceed 89.62 MHz to clear\nacceptance. A 90.5 MHz median would give 267.14 iterations/sec, up 5.6\npercent. Recheck the address, predicate, ALU and issue feedback paths\nin all scored placements and run the unchanged correctness gates.\n \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
