{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json; [(lambda x: print(x[\\\"id\\\"], x.get(\\\"title\\\"), x.get(\\\"outcome\\\"), \\\"\\\\n\\\", x.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\")))(json.loads(line)) for line in sys.stdin]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-002-r1s1 Move DIV and REM to a shared iterative execute unit regression \n {'motivation': \"Bench's baseline closed at 5.442 MHz median Fmax and 12.12 CoreMark\\niterations per second. The reported 199.837 ns, 156-cell path runs from a\\nwriteback destination through forwarding and the EX ALU toward the data\\naddress path. The four combinational division and remainder expressions\\nfeed the same result mux as ordinary ALU operations and are the likely\\nsource of the deep logic. Every instruction pays their timing cost.\\nAn unmodified baseline cosim with both bus stalls enabled reproduced\\nthe logged 4,491,485 bracketed cycles and passed CoreMark CRC validation.\\nIts 2,922,015 timed retirements included zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU\\ninstructions.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Move DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU into one blocking, clocked execute unit. At\\nlaunch, capture forwarded operands and the operation, and register the\\noperand magnitudes and sign flags. On the first update clock, compute\\nthe top radix-4 digit from the dividend magnitude's top two bits and\\nregister the divisor multiples D, 2D and 3D. Among nonzero divisors,\\nonly magnitudes one, two and three can yield a nonzero first digit. On\\neach of the next five clocks, cascade three radix-4 digit steps to\\nconsume the remaining 30 bits. A step appends two dividend bits to the\\npartial remainder and uses three parallel trial subtractions to select\\nthe largest multiple that fits. Use 34-bit divisor multiples and 35-bit\\nsubtracts to preserve the full trial value and detect borrow. Select\\nthe quotient or remainder, apply its sign and handle divisor zero and\\nsigned overflow before the existing EX/MEM transfer. Keep the result\\nstable until that transfer.\\nThis adds seven cycles with ready buses, leaving margin in the existing\\nten-cycle liveness window for a redirect or load-use bubble. Use the same\\nissue and completion schedule for ALTOPS, with the four existing\\nopcode-specific arithmetic substitutes.\\n\\nHold fetch and ID/EX for the divide, allow older MEM/WB\n…[60940 of 64012 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:707734fd61890978]…\nnges': [{'file': 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'description': \"Replace the five-way read mux into decode with a fixed head register and four circular tail slots. Preserve admission and recovery on the same cycles and expose the fixed head's raw source fields and effective validity for the interlock.\"}, {'file': 'rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'description': 'Compute load-use aliases on the queued head and bus source fields in parallel. Qualify the two hits with mutually exclusive head and bus eligibility and retain the existing load, dmem and arithmetic rules.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Connect the fixed-head and bus interlock inputs. Keep the same issue hold and squash behavior and single-channel RVFI wiring.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Cover simultaneous head issue and tail refill, full-to-space recovery, a dependent buffered head held by an EX load, and correction with an occupied queue and either a ready or unready bus. Check fetch and retirement order and wrong-path side effects.'}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
