{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(line) for line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()]\nfor row in rows[-6:]:\n print(row['id'],row['fmax_mhz'],row['cycles'],row['critical_path'],row['logic_levels'])\n print(row['implementation_notes'][:3000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r11s0 89.456 3956104 {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.pc_2_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/recovery_sequential_in_window_q_s0', 'slack_ns': -8.721, 'arrival_ns': 19.015, 'logic_cells': 12, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if']} 12\n# Bench branch recovery\n\nID registers correction qualifiers from the validated transfer class, the issued prediction, the mapped-edge marker, and direct displacement bit 1. Aligned branches carry separate direct and sequential requests for the two outcomes. At the mapped edge, a misaligned direct transfer uses the unconditional sequential request because either branch outcome resumes at PC+4. JALR resolves separately using the forwarded two-bit address sum.\n\nEX compares eight four-bit groups in parallel and combines adjacent groups by passing the lower group's ordering through only when the higher group matches. It uses the sign bits to resolve signed ordering when the signs differ and uses funct3 to choose and invert the condition. The three advancing recovery requests feed the existing IF selectors. Their union squashes ID on the same edge. EX still computes full-width PC+4 and B/J targets, and IF uses the EX/MEM address for JALR.\n\nID also qualifies misaligned JAL link suppression. EX calculates bit 1 of the JALR address sum for each forwarding source in parallel and selects it with the registered operand mask.\n\nThe predictor keeps branch and JAL predictions in separate queue bits and folds the PC candidates before selecting the table index. It registers both possible saturating counter updates beside the branch outcome. The chosen pending value still feeds both lookups on an index alias, and a taken misaligned branch suppresses both forwarding and the table write.\n\nDirected pipeline tests exercise forwarded operands across all comparison groups, both misprediction directions, mapped-edge traps and fall-through, predictor aliasing after a misaligned taken branch, and both bus stalls during recovery.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Strict Verilator lint and all seven cocotb runners\n…[11784 of 14856 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:99d8fb8ec34fc3e6]…\n Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all seven cocotb runners passed.\n- Cosim passed for selftest and all three random traces with and without stalls. Stalled CoreMark passed its canonical CRCs and completed in 3,489,822 cycles between the MMIO markers, 1.92% below the champion.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 87.528, 89.052, and 89.052 MHz. The 89.052 MHz median yields 255.18 CoreMark iterations/sec, up 0.90%. It falls below the 93 MHz target. The two median placements were limited by forwarded EX operands through the ALU result path. Later trials with a split direction table or shared right shifter reduced median Fmax, so the worktree contains the higher-scoring build.\n- Formal passed 52 of 53 checks under the unchanged single-retirement configuration. `reg_ch0` was still solving at the local wall-clock limit without a counterexample. The first run stopped at elaboration because Yosys rejected `$bits` applied to a typedef. Applying `$bits` to the packed record signal resolved the error.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
