{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git branch --show-current && git status --short && git rev-parse HEAD && rg '\\\"id\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"hyp-20261122-003-r13s2\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; r=json.load(sys.stdin); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"bench-hyp-20261122-002-r14s1-hypgen\n3b2084de2140fcc32780bc9d9b848d66b9829b08\nIf an ordinary producer word is accepted with a sequential prediction,\nalso admit the synchronous PC+8 response when its exact captured PC is\nthe accepted word's PC+4 and there is room for both words. The first\nword may be an accepted matching bus response or an accepted tagged\nreplay. Validate the RAM tag, staged read eligibility and read/write\ncollision state before admitting the second word. Take its instruction,\ndirection and successor from the same record. Store the selected\nabsolute successor and its boundary flag at fill time so the pair adds\na registered successor candidate to the producer mux. Leave the first\ncorrected fetch on the bus. Use a sequential prediction for a ready\nresponse on the registered recovery cycle and resume ordinary B/J\nprediction from the registered producer.\n\nKeep at most five buffered instructions. Use one extra payload register\nand count it against that same capacity. Admit a pair only when this\nregister is free and at most three queue slots are occupied before the\ncycle's pop. The first word takes its ordinary queue slot or empty\nbus bypass. Save the circular queue position immediately after the\nfirst word alongside the second word. If the first bypasses, save the\ncurrent write position. Register when the second word reaches the\nlogical head, based on dequeues of older words. While it is head, issue\nit before the next ring entry and hold it under issue backpressure.\nStart the next fetch at the second word's predicted successor even if\ndecode holds. The issue decision only determines the marker position\nand whether the first word queues. Select the buffered head, paired\nword and live bus word in parallel. Compare the EX load destination\nwith both raw source fields in each ring slot, the paired register\nand the live bus word in parallel. Select the active match with the\nread pointer and head eligibility, keeping the instruction payload\nmux out of the issue-to-occupancy path.\n\nExclude the last mapped word from the second position and retain\nbus-side boundary parking. If the first word came from replay on an\nunready bus, retain the held bus address and discard its eventual\nresponse under the existing stale-request rules. EX resolves both\nwords normally at single retirement width. Recovery discards the pair\nand younger queue state, including after a wrong prediction in either\nword. Reset and advancing FENCE.I clear the extra valid state as well\nas the existing replay state and cancel in-flight replay publication.\n\nA scratch prototype with pairing behind zero to three queued words\ncompleted canonical stalled CoreMark in 3,464,124 timed cycles, down\n2.64%. A simpler empty-queue-only prototype reached 83.848 MHz median\nwith queue feedback among its worst paths. Timing of the proposed\npaired admission with independent source matches is unmeasured. At\n3,464,124 cycles the median must exceed 91.64 MHz to clear the 4.6%\nthreshold. The 92.1 MHz target would give about 265.87 iterations/sec.\nCheck all three Gowin placements, canonical CRCs and the unchanged\nRVFI and formal gates. Exercise pairing with a resident older head,\nsimultaneous issue and refill, a full queue, both directions in the\nsecond word, wrong-path stores, a stale bus response and FENCE.I.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
