{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_40", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"hyp-20261121-002-r11s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; r=json.load(sys.stdin); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"", "aggregated_output": "Separate conditional branch execution from recovery. For an ordinary\nbranch, use EX to compare forwarded operands and register the outcome\nalongside the direct and sequential recovery candidates, with their\nwindow and last-word flags computed in parallel. Prequalify each\ncandidate with the prediction, target alignment and boundary status.\nOn the following cycle, use the registered outcome to select the IF\nrequest and kill the speculative ID/EX slot while ID admits the corrected\nfetch. This keeps the champion's ordinary branch redirect latency and\ntakes the live comparison off the issue enable and JALR selector.\nDerive branch trap and RVFI next PC from the same latched outcome.\nKeep JAL/JALR and non-branch boundary recovery on their current schedule.\n\nIssue a legal BEQ/BNE immediately behind a load to a matching nonzero\ndestination. Carry matching-source bits and the ordinarily forwarded\nvalues for both operands into EX/MEM. When the load leaves MEM, MEM/WB\ncaptures its formatted value through the normal path. In the branch's\nMEM cycle, substitute the registered load value for each matching\noperand only when the older load wrote its destination. For a trapped\nload, the saved forwarded values supply the old architectural register\ncontents. If both sources name the load destination, they are equal in\neither case. Keep the existing load-use interlock for other pairs and\nhold the load, branch context and forwarding values through data stalls.\nThe registered response separates the data bus from issue and recovery.\n\nAn older load-dependent branch resolves in MEM while younger work can\nadvance in EX and ID. Register its outcome and squash both younger\nslots on a correction before they can issue a memory request or retire.\nApply the registered kill before forming dmem backpressure or bypass\neligibility so the corrected instruction can issue without a false\nstall or dependency. Defer younger multicycle launch while the late\nbranch remains unresolved. Retain the previous load's MEM/WB bypass\nvalue while that branch enters W\n…[4256 of 7328 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e74dc98a200b8029]…\nlow use required clock period in nanoseconds from Gowin's setup report. No path launched by the late-correction register appeared in the 25 reported paths for any selected placement. The table gives the period of the last reported path for reference. Before the late target mux moved into MEM, placement 0 reported a 12.182 ns path from the late correction register to the IF queue occupancy.\n\n| Place option | Fmax MHz | Ordinary correction to fetch PC | Forwarded base ALU | Forwarded low multiply | Last reported path |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | 87.643 | 11.342 | 11.407 | 11.410 | 11.211 |\n| 1 | 90.538 | 11.030 | 11.044 | 11.045 | 10.885 |\n| 2 | 90.538 | 11.030 | 11.044 | 11.045 | 10.885 |\n\nThe median is 90.538 MHz and the resulting fitness is 235.35 iterations/s, 0.75% below the champion. The measured cycles require 95.418 MHz for the 4.6% acceptance margin. The forwarded low multiply is the longest reported path in all three placements, with base ALU and ordinary recovery close behind.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
