{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nS={'r12s0','r12s1','r10s1','r8s0','r7s0','r14s1'}\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].split('-')[-1] in S:\n  print(d['id'],d['title']); print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis']); print(d.get('implementation_notes','')[:4500]); print('\\\\n')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r7s0 Parallelize fetch address range and predictor index computation\nSplit PC-relative target arithmetic at bit 20, the boundary of the\n1 MiB instruction window. Add the low 18 word-address bits to the low\n18 bits of the signed B or J word offset. The carry minus the offset\nsign adjusts PC[31:20] by +1, 0 or -1. Form the full target from the\nthree page candidates and the low sum. In parallel, compare the\noriginal page with 0xfff, 0 or 1 and combine those comparisons with\nthe same adjustment to tell whether the target page is zero, including\n32-bit wraparound. Keep the target halfword alignment check. Use the\nsame page split for PC+4 and reuse the incremented page candidate.\n\nIn parallel with the request address mux, compute the existing\nsix-bit XOR hash for the predicted PC and each registered recovery\nPC. Select the hash with the same mutually exclusive masks as the\nrequest address and use it for the existing direction lookup and\npending-update check. Keep full 32-bit target arithmetic, predictor\npolicy and updates, fetch queue admission, last-word parking and EX\nrecovery priority. Preserve the current bus and retirement schedule.\n\nThe scratch build measured 80.204, 83.632 and 83.632 MHz across Gowin\nplacement options 0, 1 and 2 with 4,089 logic cells in option 0.\nLow-word multiply became the longest reported path. Stalled CoreMark\npassed the canonical CRC checks in 3,956,104 marker-bracketed cycles.\nThe measured median and cycle count imply 211.40 iter/s, 9.64 percent\nabove the champion. The acceptance floor at this cycle count is\nabout 79.79 MHz. The scratch RTL passed the existing cocotb suites,\nthe directed window-edge test and selftest plus three random ISS\ntraces with ready and stalled buses. All 53 single-retire ALTOPS\nformal checks passed. A combinational SAT check proved the target,\nrange, increment and hash equations equivalent for word-aligned\nrequests and mutually exclusive PC selectors.\n\n# Implementation notes\n\nFetch now adds the 18 word-address bits below the 1 MiB boundary. The lo\n…[22367 of 25439 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:27cd38c93f95585d]…\naken branch. The corrected fetch and a consecutive training\nlookup must see the pending update on an index alias. Preserve the\ndecode legality and alignment rules, single-channel retirement order,\nand divider completion schedule.\n\nScratch versions passed ready and stalled RVFI cosim, all seven cocotb\nsuites, and the canonical CoreMark CRC gate in 3,385,175 timed cycles,\ndown 4.86 percent. An early version reduced the predicate after the\nregister and reached 70.650 MHz median. Later builds that computed\ncandidate successors in parallel still exposed recovery through issue\nto the queue pointers. The proposed register cut and independent\nnormal ring update address those remaining paths. Timing closure on\nthe complete partition is unverified.\nAt the measured cycle count acceptance requires above 89.55 MHz median.\nA 91 MHz median would yield 268.82 iterations/sec, about 6.3 percent\nabove the champion. Treat that as a timing target. Measure all scored\nplacements and rerun the unmodified formal and cosim gates.\n\n\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
