{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-(003-r12s2|003-r11s2|003-r10s2|002-r12s1)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; [print(d[\\\"id\\\"], \\\"\\\\n\\\", d.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\"), \\\"\\\\n\\\", str(d.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\"))[-7300:]) for d in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r10s2 \n {'motivation': 'The bench champion scores 237.13 CoreMark iterations/sec at 93.81 MHz\\nand 3,956,104 timed cycles. Backedge-scoped replay previously saved\\nabout 7.7% of cycles. Tagging individual fetch predictions lets useful\\nrecords survive exits and loop changes. A 512-entry scratch build with\\none-word lookahead saved 11.12% of cycles with all five canonical CRCs.\\nIts 84.015 MHz median was limited by a block-RAM-to-next-PC path.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Record each accepted, matching bus prediction in a direct-mapped\\n1,024-entry synchronous RAM. Key it by the low ten word-PC bits and\\nstore the remaining eight in-window word-PC bits, the instruction,\\npredicted-taken bit and the 18-bit target used by the ordinary predictor.\\nStage writes and suppress same-index read/write collisions. Qualify\\nlookups with the full address identity and validity. Reuse the recorded\\ndirection for a replayed branch and let advancing EX check the actual\\ndirection and train the existing direction table. The recorded direct\\ntarget derives from the same instruction and PC. JALR prediction stays\\nsequential and its target is resolved in EX.\\n\\nLaunch the replay RAM read at PC+8 using the normal registered producer\\nPC. Pass the RAM result and its checked tag into a second register\\nbefore it can affect next-PC selection. Two sequential advances align\\nthe result with the current producer. One advance and one hold align it\\nwith the following word. Save accepted lookahead payloads in separate\\neven and odd word-PC registers. Derive payload write enables from the\\nstaged response and its saved parity. On a sequential advance, clear\\nthe consumed slot's validity and let the new PC select the following\\nslot without moving its payload. Holds retain both slots. Nonsequential\\nadvances and recovery clear both slots' validity. Skip lookahead across\\nthe instruction-window boundary. The read address depends on the\\nregistered PC and a constant increment.\\n\\nPrefer a fresh, ready bus word. Admit a replay only when that\n…[19575 of 22647 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a65e24aaa3256095]…\n Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all seven cocotb runners passed.\n- Cosim passed for selftest and all three random traces with and without stalls. Stalled CoreMark passed its canonical CRCs and completed in 3,489,822 cycles between the MMIO markers, 1.92% below the champion.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 87.528, 89.052, and 89.052 MHz. The 89.052 MHz median yields 255.18 CoreMark iterations/sec, up 0.90%. It falls below the 93 MHz target. The two median placements were limited by forwarded EX operands through the ALU result path. Later trials with a split direction table or shared right shifter reduced median Fmax, so the worktree contains the higher-scoring build.\n- Formal passed 52 of 53 checks under the unchanged single-retirement configuration. `reg_ch0` was still solving at the local wall-clock limit without a counterexample. The first run stopped at elaboration because Yosys rejected `$bits` applied to a typedef. Applying `$bits` to the packed record signal resolved the error.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
