{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_37", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-003-r9s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(d.get(\\\"reason\\\")); print(str(d.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\"))[-1500:])'\"", "aggregated_output": "Keep the champion's direction predictor for bus fetches and add four\ntrace entries sharing a 512-record synchronous RAM with one read port and\none write port. Start a capture from a registered event for an advancing,\naligned, taken conditional backedge spanning at most 128 words in the\ninstruction window. Retain completed entries for contained backedges.\nChoose an invalid entry or the least recently used entry outside active\nplayback. Exclude the entry being claimed that cycle from replacement.\nCapture one iteration from the next header through the same taken\nbackedge, using staged, advancing EX samples in execution order. Stage\nsequential and direct recovery addresses separately from the taken bit,\nthen form the recorded successor on the capture side of the registers.\nRequire a non-control header instruction and keep its encoding for the\nentry check. Reject JALR, illegal instructions, faulting control transfers,\nFENCE.I and the last mapped word. Abort on an out-of-range PC or successor,\nan early exit, or more than 128 records. Publish the entry only after the\nfinal write. Each record contains the instruction, 18-bit word PC, 18-bit\nresolved successor word PC and direct-taken bit. The terminal successor\nis the header. Warm entries remain available across loop exits.\n\nEnter replay on ordinary bus acceptance of a matching header PC and\ninstruction. Match the normal PC register against the directory in\nparallel with the bus fetch and preselect the lowest PC match on a tie.\nCheck that entry's instruction encoding only to authorize the handshake.\nUse the ordinary bus path for the first corrected fetch. Each entry\nkeeps record 1, the first instruction after the header, in flops. Load it\ninto the shared replay output register on entry. Pre-read the following\nrecord from RAM using the directory match, then qualify the result with\nthe entry handshake. The next replay cycle can supply record 1 to the\nexisting five-entry fall-through queue. Thereafter, move the RAM return\nthrough the replay output register and advance the r\n…[1580 of 4652 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1640a89af2e63a97]…\nmory and\nsingle-channel RVFI behavior.\n\nA trace model covered 1.60 million of 2.92 million timed retirements with\nfour entries, compared with 1.04 million with one. Both modeled the\nheader as a bus fetch and delayed use by five retirements after publication.\nA cycle-level Verilator proxy supplied the recorded direction and made\nmatching warm words available to the champion FIFO during imem stalls.\nIt reproduced 3,956,104 cycles with the proxy disabled and ran in\n3,662,345 cycles with replay and a five-cycle publication delay, a 7.43%\nreduction with canonical CRCs. The proxy assumes a replay word is ready\nwhenever the FIFO can admit it. FPGA timing remains unmeasured. At an\nimplementation target of 3.70 million timed cycles and 88.5 MHz,\nfitness would be 239.19 iter/sec, up 6.1%. At that cycle count the median\nneeds to exceed 87.27 MHz to clear the acceptance margin. Measure the\nactual bracketed cycles and median of the three Gowin placements, and\ncheck the canonical CRCs and existing formal gates.\n\nNone\nNone\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
