{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\":\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; rows=[json.loads(l) for l in sys.stdin]; print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(f\\\"{r[chr(105)+chr(100)]} {r.get(chr(99)+chr(121)+chr(99)+chr(108)+chr(101)+chr(115))} {r.get(chr(102)+chr(105)+chr(116)+chr(110)+chr(101)+chr(115)+chr(115))}\\\\n{r.get(chr(109)+chr(111)+chr(116)+chr(105)+chr(118)+chr(97)+chr(116)+chr(105)+chr(111)+chr(110))}\\\" for r in rows))'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r1s2 4186330 13.22\nThe bench core fetches fall-through until EX resolves a control transfer. Each\naligned taken branch or jump normally discards one fetch opportunity because\nIF feeds ID/EX without a separate IF/ID register. CoreMark repeatedly visits\nloop branches, calls, and returns. The baseline's 5.442 MHz median Fmax is\nlimited by the forwarded ALU path, so the predictor must earn its gain in\nmarker-bracketed cycles while keeping the fetch feedback path short.\n\nhyp-20261121-003-r2s2 4186330 116.68\nThe current bench champion loses one fetch slot on each aligned taken\nbranch or jump because IF feeds ID/EX directly and resolves transfers in EX.\nThe round-one 64-entry BTB reduced marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles from\n4,491,485 to 4,186,330 with both bus stalls enabled. Its Fmax was measured\non the older combinational-divider core. The iterative-divider champion\nreaches 48.196 MHz, and the other divider trial exposed an EX-to-fetch\ntiming path. Preserving that clock while removing taken-transfer bubbles\nis the opportunity for this predictor.\n\nhyp-20261121-003-r3s2 4190307 138.94\nThe current champion runs ten timed CoreMark iterations in 4,491,485 cycles\nat 54.759 MHz. The tagged BTB trial on the round-two starting RTL saved\n6.79% of cycles and scored 116.68 iter/sec at a 48.845 MHz median. The\ncurrent champion scores 121.92 iter/sec. The bench pipeline has no IF/ID\nregister, so predicting a direct transfer at fetch acceptance can still put\nthe target in front of EX on the next cycle. Deriving that target from the\nfetched instruction lets EX verify direction with one bit and removes the\ntarget RAM and full-address match network used in the prior predictor.\n\nhyp-20261121-003-r4s2 3956704 150.3\nThe champion takes 4,190,307 CoreMark cycles at 58.220 MHz. A timed trace\nhas 13,735 JALRs, and replaying the direction table estimates about 42,000\nconditional misses. Even perfect direction and return prediction would\nrecover only a small fraction of the cycles needed for acceptance. The\npredictor and fetch\n…[3730 of 6802 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d7bf1dc304b52961]…\n\nRAM also removes a wide resettable validity vector from fetch.\n\nhyp-20261121-003-r12s2 3489822 255.18\nThe champion's four replay slots are filled from a synchronous PC+8 lookup. A predicted taken branch or JAL leaves that sequential stream, so either of the first two destination words can stall on imem while the replay slots catch up. A scratch 32-entry asynchronous target buffer saved 98,048 of 3,558,019 timed cycles, but the lookup and next-PC feedback reduced median Gowin Fmax to 85.508 MHz.\n\nhyp-20261122-003-r13s2 3464124 260.06\nThe bench champion takes 3,558,019 timed CoreMark cycles at 89.983 MHz.\nIts PC+8 replay RAM often returns the following instruction in the same\ncycle that fetch admits the current one. Fetch today publishes at most\none word per cycle. Admitting the verified following record at the same\nedge can build a queue lead before the next imem stall. Round 12's extra\ndirect-target reads saved 1.92% of cycles at 89.052 MHz. Paired admission\ncan use the champion's single existing RAM read.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
